{"type":"item.started","item":{"id":"item_7","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/sh -lc \"cat postdraft_reference_retrieval.log references.openalex.bib references.openalex.json; sed -n '1,135p' paper.tex; cat validation_report.json; sed -n '1,130p' validate_artifacts.py; cat .codex/skills/validate-paper/SKILL.md; sed -n '1,90p' paper_strategy.md; cat postdraft_reference_query_plan.json\"","aggregated_output":"","exit_code":null,"status":"in_progress"}}
{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_7","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/sh -lc \"cat postdraft_reference_retrieval.log references.openalex.bib references.openalex.json; sed -n '1,135p' paper.tex; cat validation_report.json; sed -n '1,130p' validate_artifacts.py; cat .codex/skills/validate-paper/SKILL.md; sed -n '1,90p' paper_strategy.md; cat postdraft_reference_query_plan.json\"","aggregated_output":"[paper.sh] reference query plan ready: 2 queries\n[semantic_scholar] search start | query 1/2 | multivariate split conformal rectangular prediction regions maximum standardized absolute residual score\n[semantic_scholar] search complete | [------------] | refs 0/25 (25 left) | candidates 0/1 | query 1/2\n[semantic_scholar] fetching | [#-----------] | refs 1/25 (24 left) | candidates 1/1 | query 1/2\n[semantic_scholar] search start | query 2/2 | multivariate conformal prediction simultaneous prediction intervals coordinatewise Bonferroni joint maximum residual calibration\n[semantic_scholar] search complete | [#-----------] | refs 1/25 (24 left) | candidates 0/1 | query 2/2\n[semantic_scholar] fetching | [#-----------] | refs 2/25 (23 left) | candidates 1/1 | query 2/2\n[paper.sh] attempting OpenAlex fallback: Semantic Scholar retrieval did not meet minimum references: 2 < 25\n[paper.sh] OpenAlex contributed 1 references\n@misc{https://doi.org/10.48550/arxiv.2512.15383,\n  doi = {10.48550/ARXIV.2512.15383},\n  url = {https://arxiv.org/abs/2512.15383},\n  author={Fan, Yunjie and Matteo Sesia},\n  keywords = {Methodology (stat.ME), Statistics Theory (math.ST), Machine Learning (stat.ML), FOS: Computer and information sciences, FOS: Computer and information sciences, FOS: Mathematics, FOS: Mathematics},\n  title = {Interpretable Multivariate Conformal Prediction with Fast Transductive Standardization},\n  publisher = {arXiv},\n  year = {2025},\n  copyright = {Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International}\n}\n{\n  \"count\": 1,\n  \"queries\": [\n    \"multivariate split conformal rectangular prediction regions maximum standardized absolute residual score\",\n    \"multivariate conformal prediction simultaneous prediction intervals coordinatewise Bonferroni joint maximum residual calibration\"\n  ],\n  \"references\": [\n    {\n      \"citation_count\": 4,\n      \"citation_key\": \"https://doi.org/10.48550/arxiv.2512.15383\",\n      \"id\": \"ref.openalex.W7116099311\",\n      \"paper_id\": \"https://openalex.org/W7116099311\",\n      \"query\": \"multivariate split conformal rectangular prediction regions maximum standardized absolute residual score\",\n      \"retrieved_at\": \"2026-11-17T14:20:00.598502+00:00\",\n      \"source_url\": \"https://doi.org/10.48550/arxiv.2512.15383\",\n      \"title\": \"Interpretable Multivariate Conformal Prediction with Fast Transductive Standardization\",\n      \"venue\": \"arXiv (Cornell University)\",\n      \"year\": 2025\n    }\n  ],\n  \"retrieved_at\": \"2026-11-17T14:20:08.628824+00:00\",\n  \"source\": \"openalex\"\n}\\documentclass{article}\n\\usepackage{iclr2026_conference,times}\n\\usepackage{amsmath,amssymb,booktabs,graphicx,subcaption}\n\\usepackage{hyperref,url}\n\\title{Scale-Adaptive Conformal Regions:\\\\Reliability and Limits on the Simplex}\n\\author{}\n\\newcommand{\\R}{\\mathbb{R}}\n\\newcommand{\\simplex}{\\Delta^{K-1}_{\\circ}}\n\\newcommand{\\clip}{\\operatorname{clip}}\n\\begin{document}\n\\maketitle\n\n\\begin{abstract}\nPrediction regions for compositional responses must account for both the simplex constraint and variation in predictive uncertainty. We study whether scale-adaptive split-conformal regions in isometric-logratio coordinates improve reliability and efficiency together. A frozen training-only procedure combines ridge centers, local residual-moment scaling, and nested rank-based selection; independent calibration determines the final regions. Across logistic-normal, Dirichlet, and shifted-Dirichlet settings at two dimensions and eight held-out seeds, the results are condition-dependent. Mean absolute coverage error is 0.0136 under IID sampling but 0.1303 under shift. Aggregate utility exceeds the strongest standard comparator by 0.0116, with a descriptive paired 95\\% seed-bootstrap interval of [0.0047, 0.0179], yet mean uniform-simplex region mass is larger: 0.3035 versus 0.2917. Gains concentrate in higher-dimensional IID Dirichlet responses, whereas shifted comparisons favor the simplex-coordinate baseline. These findings support separating observed-response coverage, reference-measure efficiency, and distributional assumptions rather than interpreting an aggregate utility gain as uniform dominance or conditional validity.\n% @evidence[[paper_data.json#/final_evaluation/metrics]]\n% @evidence[[analysis/summary.json#/paired_overall/simplex_linf]]\n\\end{abstract}\n\n\\section{Introduction}\nCompositional predictions arise whenever the response is a nonnegative vector constrained to sum to one. A region around such a prediction should respect the constraint, cover the relevant future observation, and remain informative. These requirements are distinct. A small region in transformed coordinates need not occupy little of the simplex; a marginally calibrated region need not be reliable within a feature-defined population; and reliable IID behavior need not persist after a change in covariate distribution. The target itself also matters: uncertainty for an observed continuous composition is not the same problem as a set of possible class labels or a confidence region for an unobserved probability vector.\n\nSplit conformal prediction supplies a natural starting point because its marginal coverage guarantee separates fitting a predictive score from calibrating its threshold \\citep{Papadopoulos2002,Lei2018,Angelopoulos2021}. Compositional geometry nevertheless leaves important evaluation choices. Logratio representations remove the sum constraint but change how region size is measured \\citep{Aitchison1982,Egozcue2003}. Heteroscedastic scaling can redistribute widths without altering the calibration rule. Consequently, judging a region construction requires more than a single coverage number or a single transformed-space volume.\n\nWe ask: \\emph{how do training-only scale-adaptive logratio regions trade observed-composition coverage against uniform-simplex reference mass, relative to alternatives sharing the same fitted center?} We evaluate one fixed construction rather than claiming a comprehensive comparison of conformal methods. Its scale correction uses local moments of cross-fitted standardized residuals, with a neutral option and nested training-only selection. The final calibration sample is not used to choose that correction. The comparison includes unscaled logratio regions, a joint simplex-coordinate maximum-residual region, and coordinatewise Bonferroni regions.\n\nThe empirical answer is not uniform improvement. The scale-adaptive construction has higher aggregate utility but larger aggregate simplex mass than the simplex-coordinate baseline. The most favorable contrast occurs for IID Dirichlet responses in the larger dimension; shifted conditions provide clear adverse comparisons. Thus the useful contribution is a bounded evaluation of a specified configuration and its failure modes, not a new distribution-free guarantee. % @evidence[[analysis/summary.json]]\n\nOur contributions are: (i) an inspectable training-only scale-adaptation specification with independent finite-rank calibration; (ii) a paired synthetic assessment separating coverage, reference mass, and their utility summary; and (iii) a seed-level uncertainty analysis that retains unfavorable condition-specific comparisons and distinguishes scaling effects from untested selector-specific explanations. The scope is strictly positive continuous responses under the stated generators. Structural zeros, latent probability recovery, real-data performance, and universal geometric preferences are outside the evidence.\n\n\\begin{figure}[t]\n\\centering\n\\includegraphics[width=.98\\textwidth,height=1.6in,keepaspectratio]{figures/composition_reliability_overview.png}\n\\caption{Study overview. Training data determine centers and local scales, including internal rank-based selection. A separate calibration sample determines conformal thresholds. Evaluation distinguishes membership of observed compositions from mass under a uniform-simplex reference distribution; neither estimand establishes conditional or shifted validity.}\n\\label{fig:overview}\n% @evidence[[source_evidence/selected_model.py]]\n% @evidence[[source_evidence/evaluate_final.py]]\n\\end{figure}\n\n\\section{Related Work}\n\\paragraph{Calibration and adaptation.}\nThe conformal framework provides finite-sample predictive coverage under exchangeability \\citep{Vovk2005}; inductive constructions separate model fitting and calibration \\citep{Papadopoulos2002}. Regression studies compare the statistical and computational behavior of full and split conformal inference \\citep{Lei2018}, while jackknife+ uses leave-one-out predictions to account for fitting variability \\citep{Barber2021Jackknife}. Here the calibration procedure is ordinary split conformal: internal cross-fitting estimates scale information but is not jackknife+ calibration. Locally adaptive intervals have been developed through quantile regression \\citep{Romano2019,Sesia2020}, conditional density estimation \\citep{Lei2014,Izbicki2020}, and localized conformal constructions \\citep{Guan2023}. These motivate adaptation without establishing that our particular scalar correction is optimal. Selection and aggregation raise a separate issue from calibrating one fixed score \\citep{Yang2024}; our fitting choices are confined to training data.\n\n\\paragraph{What coverage does not imply.}\nConditional validity requires care even for inductive predictors \\citep{Vovk2012}. Distribution-free conditional predictive inference has fundamental limits \\citep{Barber2021Limits}, so pooled coverage should not be promoted to a feature-conditional guarantee. Under covariate shift, weighted conformal methods use covariate likelihood ratios \\citep{Tibshirani2019}; broader departures from exchangeability require their own analysis \\citep{Barber2023Beyond}. Adaptive conformal inference instead studies an online coverage-frequency target \\citep{Gibbs2021}. These extensions are not implemented here. Our shifted evaluation is a stress test of a fixed, unweighted calibration rule, not a comparison with shift-aware methods.\n\n\\paragraph{Composition and statistical measurement.}\nCompositional analysis treats ratios and the simplex as central objects \\citep{Aitchison1982,Pawlowsky2015}; logistic-normal distributions provide transformed-normal models on that space \\citep{Aitchison1980}. Isometric logratio (ILR) coordinates give an orthonormal representation \\citep{Egozcue2003}, but orthonormality in logratio geometry does not preserve uniform-simplex mass. Our centers use ridge regression \\citep{Hoerl1970}, and the scale correction is a shrunk kernel regression of residual moments in the spirit of \\citet{Nadaraya1964}. Uncertainty reporting uses resampling across independent seed blocks \\citep{Efron1979,Davison1997} and distinguishes those intervals from within-sample Wilson coverage intervals \\citep{Brown2001}. The contribution is the resulting controlled audit, not a claim to originate any of these ingredients.\n\n\\section{Prediction Regions and Evaluation Targets}\n\\label{sec:regions}\n\\subsection{Problem setup and calibration}\nLet $X\\in\\R^6$ and $Y\\in\\simplex=\\{y\\in(0,1)^K:\\sum_k y_k=1\\}$. We predict a region $C_\\alpha(X)$ for an independent \\emph{observed response} $Y$, at target coverage $1-\\alpha$. No class label or latent multinomial parameter is evaluated. A fixed Helmert matrix $B\\in\\R^{K\\times(K-1)}$ satisfies $B^\\top B=I$ and $B^\\top\\mathbf 1=0$; the representation is\n\\begin{equation}\n z(y)=B^\\top\\log y,\\qquad h(z)=\\operatorname{softmax}(Bz).\n\\end{equation}\nFitted center $\\widehat\\mu(x)\\in\\R^{K-1}$ and positive scales $\\widehat s_j(x)$ define the nonconformity score\n\\begin{equation}\n A(x,y)=\\max_{1\\leq j<K}\\frac{|z_j(y)-\\widehat\\mu_j(x)|}{\\widehat s_j(x)}.\n \\label{eq:score}\n\\end{equation}\n% @evidence[[source_evidence/selected_model.py#_basis]]\n% @evidence[[source_evidence/evaluate_final.py#method_records]]\nGiven $m$ independent calibration scores, use the $r$th smallest score with $r=\\lceil(m+1)(1-\\alpha)\\rceil$, or $+\\infty$ if $r>m$. The region consists of positive compositions satisfying $A(x,y)\\leq q_\\alpha$. It is an axis-aligned box in the fixed ILR basis, mapped back to the simplex; it is not a full-covariance ellipsoid. The finite-rank rule, including its infinite-threshold case, is owned by the evaluation code rather than the fitted predictor. % @evidence[[source_evidence/evaluate_final.py#quantile]]\n\nConditional on the training data, exchangeable calibration and test scores give $\\Pr\\{Y\\in C_\\alpha(X)\\}\\geq1-\\alpha$ by the standard conformal rank argument \\citep{Lei2018}. Ties are handled conservatively by the non-strict membership inequality. This is a statement about a random calibration sample and future response, not a guarantee for every realized test set, every fixed feature vector, or every group. Training-only adaptation leaves this argument intact because the final calibration responses do not enter score selection. The argument does not apply unchanged to our shifted test sample.\n\n\\subsection{Reliability, mass, and utility}\nFor each fitted instance, empirical coverage $\\widehat c_\\alpha$ is the fraction of test responses inside the region. Reliability error is $e_\\alpha=|\\widehat c_\\alpha-(1-\\alpha)|$, treating overcoverage and undercoverage symmetrically. Efficiency is the normalized uniform-simplex reference mass\n\\begin{equation}\n M_\\alpha=\\mathbb E_{X_{\\rm test}}\\Pr_{V\\sim\\operatorname{Dirichlet}(\\mathbf1)}\n \\{V\\in C_\\alpha(X_{\\rm test})\\}.\n \\label{eq:mass}\n\\end{equation}\nThe implementation estimates this probability with shared probes and test contexts, not by integrating ILR-box volume. Thus $M_\\alpha\\in[0,1]$ is dimension-normalized simplex volume, not probability content under the response law. We use ``mass'' to keep the reference distribution explicit. Small mass alone does not establish informative calibrated prediction. % @evidence[[source_evidence/evaluate_final.py#generate_arrays]]\n\nThe supplied utility combines the two empirical quantities:\n\\begin{equation}\n U_\\alpha=(1-\\widehat M_\\alpha)\n \\max\\left\\{0,1-\\frac{e_\\alpha}{1-\\alpha}\\right\\}.\n \\label{eq:utility}\n\\end{equation}\nIt rewards small regions and penalizes coverage error but is not a coverage constraint. A method can improve $U_\\alpha$ by trading mass against error; its ordering need not agree with either component. We therefore report all components and treat utility as a supplied descriptive summary, not a proof of scientific superiority. Overall utility averages conditions and nominal levels equally. IID and shifted errors are averaged separately, after taking absolute deviations within each seed, condition, and level. Taking the absolute value after averaging coverage would produce a different estimand. % @evidence[[source_evidence/evaluate_final.py#aggregate]]\n\n\\subsection{Compared region constructions}\n\\textbf{Scaled ILR} uses Eq.~\\eqref{eq:score} with the fitted scales described below. \\textbf{Unscaled ILR} retains exactly the same center and basis but sets every scale to one; it removes the entire scaling package, not only the local correction. \\textbf{Simplex max} maps the common center through $h$ and calibrates $\\max_k|y_k-h_k(\\widehat\\mu(x))|$. \\textbf{Bonferroni} calibrates separate absolute residuals in each simplex coordinate at miscoverage $\\alpha/K$ and intersects the resulting intervals with the simplex. The latter is a simultaneous-coordinate baseline, not a collection of independently claimed marginal intervals. All thresholds use their own appropriate calibration residuals from the same calibration split. % @evidence[[source_evidence/evaluate_final.py#method_records]]\n\nThese comparisons hold center predictions fixed while changing the residual geometry or scaling. The unscaled comparison can support conclusions about the complete scaling construction within this evaluation. It cannot identify the effect of local smoothing, cross-fitting, shrinkage, or the selector individually. The simplex comparison additionally changes coordinates, so any observed contrast is a comparison between region constructions rather than a causal estimate for one isolated component.\n\n\\section{Method: Training-Only Scale Adaptation}\n\\label{sec:method}\n\\subsection{Rationale and base fits}\nThe design hypothesis is that feature-dependent residual variability makes constant-width ILR regions inefficient, and that a bounded, shrunk correction may allocate widths more usefully. The counter-hypothesis is equally important: a correction selected using a transformed-space size objective may not reduce simplex mass, especially under extrapolation. The study evaluates these possibilities through paired region comparisons, not through a claim of optimal scale estimation.\n\nThe design vector is $\\phi(x)=(1,x,x^{\\odot2},\\sin x,x_0x_1)$, with coordinatewise squares and sine terms. Ridge regression of $z(Y)$ on $\\phi(X)$ uses penalty 1. A second ridge fit, with penalty 10, predicts $\\log\\max(|z_j(Y)-\\widehat\\mu_j(X)|,0.02)$ from the same design. These base-scale residuals are in-sample; cross-fitting is used for the subsequent moment correction, not retroactively for the base log-residual fit. The intercept penalty is $10^{-8}$. Predicted centers are clipped to $[-100,100]$ and base scales to $[10^{-4},100]$. These are numerical bounds, not distributional assumptions. % @evidence[[source_evidence/selected_model.py#_features]]\n\n\\subsection{Local residual moments and bounded shrinkage}\nDeterministic three-fold cross-fitting within training data yields held-out centers $\\widehat\\mu^{(-i)}$ and base scales $\\widehat s^{0,(-i)}$. For each training row define\n\\begin{equation}\n v_i=\\frac{1}{K-1}\\sum_{j=1}^{K-1}\n \\left(\\frac{z_j(Y_i)-\\widehat\\mu_j^{(-i)}(X_i)}{\\widehat s_j^{0,(-i)}(X_i)}\\right)^2.\n\\end{equation}\nTraining-only feature means and standard deviations standardize the covariates. With standardized distance $d_i(x)$, Gaussian weights $w_i(x)=\\exp\\{-d_i(x)^2/(2b^2)\\}$ yield\n\\begin{equation}\n R(x)=\\frac{\\sum_iw_i(x)v_i+\\lambda}{\\sum_iw_i(x)+\\lambda},\\qquad\n a(x)=\\exp\\!\\left\\{\\frac{\\beta}{2}\\log\\clip(R(x),1/4,4)\\right\\}.\n \\label{eq:correction}\n\\end{equation}\nThe final scales are $\\widehat s_j(x)=a(x)\\widehat s^0_j(x)$, with a common row-scalar further bounded to respect the allowed scale range. Shrinkage returns $R(x)$ toward one when kernel support is weak; $\\beta=0$ is the neutral correction. The scalar leaves relative ILR-coordinate scales unchanged at a given $x$. It does not learn new directions or correlations. % @evidence[[source_evidence/selected_model.py#build_oof_residual_table]]\n\n\\subsection{Nested rank-based selection}\nThe finite family uses $\\beta\\in\\{0,0.5,1\\}$, $b\\in\\{0.5\\sqrt6,\\sqrt6\\}$, and $\\lambda\\in\\{10,50\\}$. For each of three outer training folds, the remaining rows fit both the base model and an inner cross-fitted moment table. The held-out fold is split into two halves, used in both orientations as pseudo-calibration and evaluation data. Each pseudo-calibration half gives a finite-rank radius $q_{f,\\alpha}$ at the same three miscoverage levels used for reporting. The selector minimizes the evaluation-row-weighted, level-averaged objective\n\\begin{equation}\n L(\\theta)=\\sum_f\\frac{|E_f|}{n_{\\rm train}}\\frac{1}{|\\mathcal A|}\n \\sum_{\\alpha\\in\\mathcal A}\\log\\left[\n \\frac1{|E_f|}\\sum_{i\\in E_f}(2q_{f,\\alpha})^{K-1}\n \\prod_{j=1}^{K-1}\\widehat s_{f,j,\\theta}(X_i)\\right],\n \\label{eq:selection}\n\\end{equation}\nwhere $f$ indexes both orientations and $\\mathcal A=\\{0.05,0.10,0.20\\}$. The logarithm is applied to arithmetic mean box volume, evaluated stably, not to each row before averaging. Ties favor smaller $\\beta$. The selected correction is refitted using a full training moment table. % @evidence[[source_evidence/selected_model.py#_select_correction]]\n\nCrucially, Eq.~\\eqref{eq:selection} measures \\emph{ILR-coordinate box volume}. The nonlinear inverse map does not have a constant simplex-volume Jacobian, so this is not Eq.~\\eqref{eq:mass}. A uniform multiplicative rescaling of all scales cancels against its conformal radius in the absence of clipping; improvement must come from changing allocation across inputs or coordinates, not merely multiplying all widths by a constant. The supplement derives this algebraic check. Neither property establishes that the selected correction improves the final mass or coverage.\n\nUnavailable finite ranks, nonpositive radii, or nonfinite objectives trigger a training-only Gaussian-loss selector over the same scale family; very small samples use a neutral correction. These are source-defined fallbacks. The retained results do not record how often each fallback was used or which setting was selected in each fit. We do not infer those frequencies from aggregate performance. Independent final calibration remains unchanged in every branch. % @evidence[[source_evidence/selected_model.py#_select_correction_gaussian]]\n\n\\section{Experimental Design and Uncertainty}\n\\label{sec:design}\n\\subsection{Synthetic conditions and held-out protocol}\nThe available evidence covers one continuous-composition task, with six conditions: logistic-normal IID, Dirichlet IID, and shifted Dirichlet, each at $K\\in\\{3,8\\}$. Every condition uses six covariates, 1,024 training observations, 512 calibration observations, and 2,048 test observations. The held-out seeds are 1009, 1013, 1019, 1021, 1031, 1033, 1039, and 1049, with $\\alpha\\in\\{0.05,0.10,0.20\\}$. Each seed is a joint repeated unit containing all conditions and nominal levels, not eighteen independent replications. All recorded condition evaluations are complete. % @evidence[[paper_data.json#/final_evaluation/evaluation_evidence/episodes]]\n% @evidence[[source_evidence/data_manifest.json]]\n\nFor IID samples, $X$ is uniform on $[-1.5,1.5]^6$. Let $W$ be the fixed sinusoidal coefficient matrix specified in the supplement, and define\n\\begin{equation}\n \\eta_j(x)=0.7\\sin((x^\\top W)_j)+0.3x_0\\cos j,\n \\qquad j=0,\\ldots,K-2.\n\\end{equation}\n{\n  \"status\": \"independent_artifact_checks_passed\",\n  \"hard_errors\": [],\n  \"scope\": \"Local source, canonical schemas, rendered PDF, retained-metric consistency and evidence links; not a claim that unavailable parent orchestration checks ran.\",\n  \"body_pages\": 9,\n  \"total_pages\": 16,\n  \"references_start_page\": 10,\n  \"supplement_start_page\": 12,\n  \"reference_count\": 25,\n  \"claim_count\": 102,\n  \"figure_count\": 4,\n  \"dangling_evidence_references\": 0,\n  \"last_body_content_bounds_pt\": [\n    84.17748260498047,\n    572.6866455078125\n  ],\n  \"last_body_fill_note\": \"Bounds are descriptive, not the authoritative parent geometric-occupancy verdict. Body has nine pages, so the special eight-page minimum-fill condition is not invoked.\",\n  \"compiled_inputs_union_check\": {\n    \"passed\": true,\n    \"recorded_input_count\": 262\n  },\n  \"qualification\": {\n    \"status\": \"completed\",\n    \"outcome\": \"qualified\",\n    \"correct\": true\n  },\n  \"environment_limitations\": [\n    \"Canonical validate_figures.py cannot import src.orchestrator.paper_step in this isolated workspace. The shipped canonical figure schema, required figure IDs, source paths and strategy density budgets were checked independently.\",\n    \"Canonical compile_latex.sh input guard rejects /usr/share/texmf/web2c/texmf.cnf because it is a system symlink into a different approved system tree. No validator or system symlink was modified. Sanitized pdflatex/bibtex passes compiled the PDF; all recorded inputs independently resolve inside the workspace or the union of approved system TeX roots.\"\n  ],\n  \"orchestrator_pending\": [\n    \"Replace the intentional conceptual-overview placeholder and prompt-log stub with the synchronous concept render, then perform the parent final compile and authoritative layout checks.\"\n  ]\n}import hashlib\nimport importlib.util\nimport json\nimport re\nimport subprocess\nfrom pathlib import Path\n\n\nimport jsonschema\nimport pymupdf\n\n\ndef main():\n    root = Path(__file__).resolve().parent\n    manifest = json.loads(Path('figure_manifest.json').read_text())\n    schema = json.loads(Path('.codex/skills/validate-paper/templates/figure_manifest.schema.json').read_text())\n    jsonschema.validate(manifest, schema)\n    strategy = json.loads(Path('paper_strategy.json').read_text())\n    planned = {figure['figure_id']: figure for figure in strategy['figure_specs']}\n    actual = {figure['figure_id']: figure for figure in manifest['figures']}\n    for figure_id, specification in planned.items():\n        assert figure_id in actual\n        figure = actual[figure_id]\n        assert figure['panel_count'] <= specification['max_panels']\n        assert all(panel['category_count'] <= specification['max_categories_per_panel'] and\n                   panel['series_count'] <= specification['max_series_per_panel'] for panel in figure['panels'])\n        assert figure['placement'] == 'full_width'\n    tex = Path('paper.tex').read_text()\n    graphics = set(re.findall(r'\\\\includegraphics(?:\\[[^\\]]*\\])?\\{([^}]+)\\}', tex))\n    registered = {path for figure in manifest['figures'] for path in figure['files'].values()}\n    assert graphics <= registered\n    assert all(Path(path).is_file() for path in registered)\n    for path in ('analysis/coverage_table.tex', 'analysis/complete_comparisons.tex'):\n        assert Path(path).read_text().strip() in tex\n    bibliography_keys = set(re.findall(r'@\\w+\\{([^,]+),', Path('references.bib').read_text()))\n    assert 25 <= len(bibliography_keys) <= 30\n    cited = {key.strip() for group in re.findall(r'\\\\cite\\w*\\{([^}]+)\\}', tex) for key in group.split(',')}\n    assert cited == bibliography_keys\n    provenance = json.loads(Path('references_provenance.json').read_text())\n    assert cited == {reference['citation_key'] for reference in provenance['references']}\n    assert all(Path(reference['read_artifact']).is_file() for reference in provenance['references'])\n    claims = json.loads(Path('claims_grounding.json').read_text())['claims']\n    evidence_anchor = '@evidence[[paper_data.json#/final_evaluation/evaluation_evidence/episodes]]'\n    payload = json.loads(Path('paper_data.json').read_text())\n    summary = json.loads(Path('analysis/summary.json').read_text())\n    for metric, summary_key in (('iid_coverage_error', 'iid_error'), ('shift_coverage_error', 'shift_error'),\n                                ('mean_volume', 'mass'), ('final_score', 'utility')):\n        assert abs(payload['final_evaluation']['metrics'][metric] - summary['methods']['scaled_ilr'][summary_key]) < 1e-12\n    assert abs(payload['final_evaluation']['metrics']['baseline_utility_gap'] -\n               summary['paired_overall']['simplex_linf']['mean']) < 1e-12\n    for key in ('status', 'outcome', 'correct'):\n        assert summary['qualification'][key] == payload['final_evaluation'][key]\n    selected_path = Path('results/ideas/03_training_only_conformal_rank_scale_selection/program_1/src/program.py')\n    assert Path('source_evidence/selected_model.py').read_bytes() == selected_path.read_bytes()\n    assert Path('source_evidence/evaluate_final.py').read_bytes() == Path('task/evaluate_final.py').read_bytes()\n    registry = set(payload['grounding_sources']) | {reference['id'] for reference in provenance['references']}\n    assert all(set(claim['source_ids']) <= registry for claim in claims)\n    summary = json.loads(Path('analysis/summary.json').read_text())\n    final = payload['final_evaluation']\n    for name, derived in [('iid_coverage_error', 'iid_error'), ('shift_coverage_error', 'shift_error'),\n                          ('mean_volume', 'mass'), ('final_score', 'utility')]:\n        assert abs(final['metrics'][name] - summary['methods']['scaled_ilr'][derived]) < 1e-12\n    assert summary['qualification'] == {key: final[key] for key in ('status', 'outcome', 'correct')}\n    assert final['outcome'] == 'qualified'\n    selected_source = Path('results/ideas/03_training_only_conformal_rank_scale_selection/program_1/src/program.py')\n    assert selected_source.read_bytes() == Path('source_evidence/selected_model.py').read_bytes()\n    assert Path('task/evaluate_final.py').read_bytes() == Path('source_evidence/evaluate_final.py').read_bytes()\n    verified = json.loads(Path('scientific-evidence/verification-report.json').read_text())\n    assert verified['summary']['dangling_references'] == 0\n    document = pymupdf.open('paper.pdf')\n    texts = [page.get_text() for page in document]\n    reference_index = next(index for index, text in enumerate(texts) if re.search(r'(?m)^REFERENCES\\s*$', text))\n    supplement_index = next(index for index, text in enumerate(texts) if 'SUPPLEMENTARY INFORMATION' in text)\n    assert 8 <= reference_index <= 9\n    assert supplement_index > reference_index\n    visible = '\\n'.join(texts)\n    prohibited = ('idea', 'ideas', 'program', 'programs', 'attempt', 'attempts', 'eligible', 'combined_score',\n                  'rubric', 'worker_01', 'worker_02')\n    assert not any(re.search(r'\\b' + token + r'\\b', visible, re.I) for token in prohibited)\n    assert 'LLM USAGE' in visible\n    assert 'Anonymous authors' in texts[0]\n    assert not re.search(r'Overfull \\\\[hv]box|undefined', Path('paper.log').read_text(), re.I)\n    validator_path = Path('.codex/skills/validate-paper/scripts/validate_rendered_si.py')\n    specification = importlib.util.spec_from_file_location('rendered_si', validator_path)\n    validator = importlib.util.module_from_spec(specification)\n    specification.loader.exec_module(validator)\n    extracted = subprocess.run(['pdftotext', '-layout', 'paper.pdf', '-'], check=True, capture_output=True, text=True)\n    assert not validator.validate_rendered_supplementary_information(extracted.stdout.split('\\f'))\n    recorded_inputs = []\n    system_roots = [Path(path).resolve() for path in ('/etc/texmf', '/usr/share/texmf', '/usr/share/texlive', '/var/lib/texmf')]\n    for line in Path('paper.fls').read_text().splitlines():\n        if not line.startswith('INPUT '):\n            continue\n        resolved = Path(line[6:]).resolve()\n        assert resolved.is_relative_to(root) or any(resolved.is_relative_to(system_root) for system_root in system_roots), str(resolved)\n        recorded_inputs.append(str(resolved))\n    last_body = document[reference_index-1]\n    bounds = [span['bbox'] for block in last_body.get_text('dict')['blocks'] if block['type'] == 0\n              for line in block['lines'] for span in line['spans']\n              if span['bbox'][0] >= 107 and 65 < span['bbox'][1] < 715]\n    report = {\n        'status': 'independent_artifact_checks_passed', 'hard_errors': [],\n        'scope': 'Local source, canonical schemas, rendered PDF, retained-metric consistency and evidence links; not a claim that unavailable parent orchestration checks ran.',\n        'body_pages': reference_index, 'total_pages': len(document),\n        'references_start_page': reference_index+1, 'supplement_start_page': supplement_index+1,\n        'reference_count': len(cited), 'claim_count': len(claims), 'figure_count': len(actual),\n        'dangling_evidence_references': verified['summary']['dangling_references'],\n        'last_body_content_bounds_pt': [min(bound[1] for bound in bounds), max(bound[3] for bound in bounds)],\n        'last_body_fill_note': 'Bounds are descriptive, not the authoritative parent geometric-occupancy verdict. Body has nine pages, so the special eight-page minimum-fill condition is not invoked.',\n        'compiled_inputs_union_check': {'passed': True, 'recorded_input_count': len(recorded_inputs)},\n        'qualification': summary['qualification'],\n        'environment_limitations': [\n            'Canonical validate_figures.py cannot import src.orchestrator.paper_step in this isolated workspace. The shipped canonical figure schema, required figure IDs, source paths and strategy density budgets were checked independently.',\n            'Canonical compile_latex.sh input guard rejects /usr/share/texmf/web2c/texmf.cnf because it is a system symlink into a different approved system tree. No validator or system symlink was modified. Sanitized pdflatex/bibtex passes compiled the PDF; all recorded inputs independently resolve inside the workspace or the union of approved system TeX roots.'\n        ],\n        'orchestrator_pending': ['Replace the intentional conceptual-overview placeholder and prompt-log stub with the synchronous concept render, then perform the parent final compile and authoritative layout checks.']\n    }\n    Path('validation_report.json').write_text(json.dumps(report, indent=2))\n    print(json.dumps(report, indent=2))\n    template_source = 'https://raw.githubusercontent.com/ICLR/Master-Template/master/iclr2026/'\n    Path('template_provenance.json').write_text(json.dumps({\n        'venue': 'ICLR 2026', 'anonymous_review': True,\n        'files': [{'path': name, 'source_url': template_source+name,\n                   'sha256': hashlib.sha256(Path(name).read_bytes()).hexdigest()}\n                  for name in ('iclr2026_conference.sty', 'iclr2026_conference.bst')]}, indent=2))\n    for name, kind, sources in [\n        ('paper.tex', 'manuscript', ['paper_data.json', 'claims_grounding.json', 'references.bib', 'source_evidence/selected_model.py', 'source_evidence/evaluate_final.py']),\n        ('paper.pdf', 'manuscript', ['paper.tex', 'references.bib', 'figure_manifest.json']),\n        ('figure_manifest.json', 'derived', ['paper_strategy.json', 'build_analysis.py']),\n        ('validation_report.json', 'derived', ['paper.tex', 'paper.pdf', 'scientific-evidence/verification-report.json'])]:\n---\nname: validate-paper\ndescription: Validate grounded paper artifacts and compile LaTeX PDF with strict fail-fast checks.\n---\n\n# What the skill is about\nRun final quality gate for the generated paper.\n\n# When to use the skill\n- After `paper.tex`, `references.bib`, and `claims_grounding.json` are written.\n- Before declaring final paper success.\n\n# What to do\n1. Validate sections and citation keys:\n```bash\npython scripts/validate_tex_refs.py \\\n  --tex paper.tex \\\n  --bib references.bib\n```\nRequire a substantive embedded Supplementary Information section after the bibliography. It must\nuse `\\\\appendix` and `\\\\section{Supplementary Information}`, contain grounded visible content plus\nat least one subsection, table, or figure, and contain no template placeholder text.\n2. Validate claim mappings, bibliography/provenance consistency, and every `@evidence[[...]]` link in the `scientific-evidence/` bundle:\n```bash\npython ../write-paper/scripts/validate_claims.py \\\n  --paper-data paper_data.json \\\n  --references references_provenance.json \\\n  --bib references.bib \\\n  --claims claims_grounding.json \\\n  --paper-dir .\n```\nThis delegates to the `scientific-evidence` skill verifier, which classifies every `@evidence[[...]]` link as `resolved`, `anchored`, or `dangling`. Any `dangling` link (or an `@evidence[[...]]` token with no `scientific-evidence/` bundle present) is a hard error and must be sent back to the paper-writing agent for repair.\n3. Validate figure manifest and figure readability budget when `figure_manifest.json` is present or `paper_strategy.json` requires figures. The canonical schema is `templates/figure_manifest.schema.json`.\n```bash\npython .claude/skills/validate-paper/scripts/validate_figures.py \\\n  --paper-dir .\n```\n4. Compile:\n```bash\nbash scripts/compile_latex.sh paper.tex\n```\nThe compile script also validates that Supplementary Information is visibly rendered on a page\nafter References with substantive content. Source-only structure checks are not sufficient for\nconditional or literal LaTeX constructs.\nIt records and validates every LaTeX input, restricts BibTeX to the canonical `references.bib`,\nand removes inherited Kpathsea path overrides before compiling.\n5. Require `paper.pdf` to exist.\n6. When `paper_data.json.paper_profile == \"standalone\"`, also fail on visible paper language that leaks pipeline terms such as `eligible`, `correct and eligible`, `correctness rate`, `worker_01`, `worker_02`, `combined_score`, or similar internal wording.\n7. When the selected format or venue guidance requires real author metadata, also fail on anonymous or generic placeholder author blocks and emit soft warnings when title/abstract/introduction/conclusion lack venue-relevant framing or when the discussion drifts into unrelated application narratives.\n8. Fail hard on any missing grounding link, missing section, missing/empty/misordered Supplementary Information, unresolved citation, bibliography/provenance mismatch, figure-manifest mismatch, density-budget violation, page-limit violation for formats with enforced budgets, standalone-language violation, author-block violation required by format/venue guidance, dangling `@evidence[[...]]` link, or compile error.\n9. Treat the parent orchestrator's validation report as authoritative during repair rounds. Last-body-page fill is rendered geometric vertical occupancy computed from text and graphics bounds, not character count or word density. Do not claim that page validation passed using a character-count proxy. If the canonical validator is unavailable in the agent workspace, make the targeted repair and return control so the parent can compile and revalidate it.\n# Paper Strategy\n\n## Decision\n\n**Status:** ready for `paper_standalone`, using `write-paper-standalone` for the later manuscript stage. This is a plan, not a paper draft.\n\n**Provisional title:** Scale-Adaptive Compositional Conformal Regions: Reliability and Limits\n\n**Central question:** Across the tested synthetic response distributions, dimensions, and covariate regimes, how do training-only scale-adaptive ILR conformal regions trade observed-composition coverage against uniform-simplex region mass relative to matched-center alternatives?\n\n**Central thesis:** In the six tested synthetic settings, scale-adaptive ILR regions exhibit condition-dependent reliability–efficiency trade-offs rather than uniform dominance: near-target IID coverage coexists with shifted Dirichlet undercoverage, and higher aggregate utility does not imply smaller simplex regions or conditional validity.\n\nThe paper is one controlled empirical study of a fixed region family. Logistic-normal IID, Dirichlet IID, and shifted Dirichlet conditions, each at K=3 and K=8, are evidence blocks under the same question—not sequential experiments or a claim of transfer to arbitrary domains. The scientific value is a reproducible method specification and a reliability–efficiency assessment with concrete failures, not a claim that a small aggregate gain establishes a new universally superior method.\n\n## Framing Decision and Alternatives\n\nNo edit context, seeded prior manuscript, `aggregated/`, or `extra_tasks/` bundle is present. The JSON decision `recentered` means narrowing the original broad task and proposed geometry audit to actual evaluated evidence; it does not assert that a prior paper was rewritten.\n\n| Candidate framing | Decision and grounded reason |\n| --- | --- |\n| Preserve an earlier broad geometry/boundary audit with local updates | Reject. No prior manuscript is available, and Hellinger/Euclidean geometry comparisons, structural-zero face audits, and Mondrian calibration were proposed rather than demonstrated. |\n| Broaden into a cross-setting conformal method family | Retain only within the six synthetic conditions sharing the fixed ILR construction. No real-data, architecture, classification-label, or alternative-geometry transfer evidence supports a larger family claim. |\n| Recenter on rank-selector superiority | Reject as the central thesis. Source code supports the mechanism, but the final evidence lacks a matched Gaussian-versus-rank selector ablation. Search-plane configurations do not establish a causal final-plane gain. |\n| Recenter on reliability–efficiency trade-offs of the frozen scale-adaptive regions | Select. Final paired comparisons, independent calibration, multiple nominal levels, and uncertainty records support both favorable IID behavior and adverse shifted results. |\n\nInspecting the selected source materially narrows the story: fixed Helmert ILR ridge centers, positive diagonal base scales, a bounded covariate-dependent scalar local-moment correction, and nested training-only rank/ILR-box-volume selection. The scalar correction is common to the ILR coordinates for a given covariate vector; it is not a separately learned full covariance geometry.\n\n## Final Evidence and Qualification\n\nRead `paper_data.json.final_evaluation` independently of any campaign summary. `downstream_study` and `study.*` grounding entries are absent. The final qualification sources explicitly report:\n\n- `final.qualification.status`: `completed`.\n- `final.qualification.outcome`: `qualified`.\n- `final.qualification.correct`: `true`.\n\nThere is no failed qualification to disclose in this run. Qualification nevertheless does not establish superiority, conditional coverage, or generalization. If authoritative later artifacts report failed qualification, preserve that outcome and its limitations in writing, review, audit, and revision. Attribute a failure to statistical performance, validity, or adaptivity only when evaluator diagnostics identify the cause; missing gate diagnostics remain unknown.\n\n`final.evidence.episodes` contains eight final seed blocks, with six completed cases per seed and three alpha values per case. Final seeds are 1009, 1013, 1019, 1021, 1031, 1033, 1039, and 1049. Nominal coverage levels are 0.95, 0.90, and 0.80. This is not a set of independent case-by-alpha replications: seeds are the outer repeated unit. The identical uncertainty summary repeated under each seed wrapper must be read once.\n\nImportant grounded anchors:\n\n| Quantity | Value and grounding |\n| --- | --- |\n| Studied method's mean IID absolute coverage error | 0.013562011718750005; `final.metric.iid_coverage_error` |\n| Studied method's mean shifted absolute coverage error | 0.13034261067708333; `final.metric.shift_coverage_error` |\n| Studied method's mean uniform-simplex mass | 0.3034934467739529; `final.metric.mean_volume` |\n| Studied method's mean utility | 0.661819668104677; `final.metric.final_score` |\n| Gap to the largest aggregate utility among standard evaluator comparators | 0.011594669497844157; `final.metric.baseline_utility_gap` |\n| Simplex-coordinate L-infinity mean mass | Approximately 0.291701, derived from evaluator method records under `final.evidence.episodes` |\n\nThese are retained measurements, not certification of a significant aggregate advantage. In particular, larger mean mass than the simplex-coordinate comparator contradicts a blanket size-improvement claim. Absolute coverage error is not signed undercoverage; signed coverage must be shown separately. The existing overall score interval cannot establish a confidence interval for the difference between methods.\n\nThe wrapper labels candidate diagnostics as unverified. Use evaluator-owned membership and method statistics, not candidate assertions. Preserve evaluator/program/result hashes in the audit trail. The available selected source is linked by configuration records; do not assert that a locally recovered final source tree has been independently rehashed when it has not.\n\n## Method Groups and Comparison Roles\n\n1. **Training-only scale-adaptive ILR regions — studied/proposed configuration.** Explain the center, base scale, correction family, training-only selector, and independent pooled calibration. “Proposed” is an organizational role, not a claim of unique novelty.\n2. **Unscaled ILR — matched-center ablation.** Set scales to one, retaining the same center and ILR basis. This compares the whole scaling package against no scaling; it does not isolate the correction or selector.\n3. **Simplex-coordinate L-infinity — primary direct baseline.** Use the inverse-ILR center and a joint maximum-coordinate residual. Include all comparisons, not only conditions favoring the studied method. Its largest aggregate utility among standard comparators is an observed result, not a preregistered comparator-selection rule.\n4. **Coordinatewise Bonferroni — simultaneous-coordinate baseline.** Use per-coordinate ranks at alpha/K. Include in the main summary table and complete supplemental comparisons.\n5. **Feature-information and degenerate-region controls — supporting diagnostics.** Constant-center, constant-input, permuted-input, whole-simplex, and zero-radius records assess feature information and metric endpoints. They are not additional deployable method families or conditional-validity evidence.\n\nThe original initializer source exists, but matched final performance records for it do not. Omit that performance comparison; do not relabel the unscaled ablation as the original initializer. Describe the archived joint-center/scale and Gaussian-selector configurations only in supplementary provenance as search-only alternatives, not final ablations. Without `study.*`, omit campaign winner claims, search-score margins, and scored/unscored accounting. Missing measurements must never become measured losses.\n\n## Scientific Organization and Results Views\n\nUse these body sections:\n\n1. Introduction.\n2. Related Work.\n3. Prediction Regions and Evaluation Targets.\n4. Training-only Scale Adaptation.\n5. Experimental Design and Uncertainty.\n6. Reliability–Efficiency Results.\n7. Discussion and Limitations.\n8. Conclusion.\n\nThe contribution list should be limited to a reimplementable training-only configuration, a matched-center evaluation separating reliability from region mass, and empirical condition-dependent findings with failure diagnostics. No new conformal theorem, comprehensive benchmark, or causal selector improvement is established.\n\nThe main results should answer three scientific questions:\n\n- **Does measured coverage track the target?** Show all nominal levels, with IID and shifted conditions separate. Shift failure belongs in the body, not only the supplement.\n- **When does scaling change the trade-off?** The IID Dirichlet K=8 comparison is informative, but retain the logistic-normal K=3 counterexample and shifted Dirichlet utility reversals. No uniform dominance claim survives those comparisons.\n- **Does a higher utility mean smaller or more reliable regions?** No. Show separate coverage and mass alongside utility. Explain that the training selector minimizes an ILR-box surrogate, whereas evaluation uses uniform-simplex mass. Different measures are factual; their causal contribution to the observed results is unresolved.\n\nInclude one compact main table with four method rows and columns for IID absolute coverage error, shifted absolute coverage error, mean reference mass, and utility. Recompute baseline aggregates from the final records using saved code. Equal weighting is across seeds, cases, and nominal levels, not a pooled mixture weighted by subgroup sample counts. Define utility rather than using an internal score label:\n\n`U = (1 - V) max(0, 1 - |coverage - (1-alpha)| / (1-alpha))`.\n\nIts error penalty includes overcoverage; it is a benchmark-specific summary, not a validity test or a standardized universal objective. Do not use a thresholded success-rate figure.\n{\n  \"status\": \"ready\",\n  \"central_topic\": \"Targeted multivariate conformal comparator context for the joint simplex-coordinate maximum-residual and coordinatewise Bonferroni regions already compared in a study of scale-adaptive split-conformal prediction for observed positive continuous compositions.\",\n  \"core_methods\": [\n    \"Independent split-conformal calibration of maximum standardized ILR residuals; existing general conformal and compositional references suffice, with no new core-method retrieval requested\"\n  ],\n  \"comparators\": [\n    \"Joint simplex-coordinate maximum absolute residual calibration, giving a common-radius rectangular region intersected with the simplex\",\n    \"Separate simplex-coordinate absolute residual calibration at miscoverage alpha/K, giving Bonferroni simultaneous intervals intersected with the simplex\"\n  ],\n  \"benchmarks\": [\n    \"Existing synthetic logistic-normal and Dirichlet IID and shifted evaluations provide context only; the gap artifacts identify no benchmark or dataset citation gap\"\n  ],\n  \"architectural_shifts\": [],\n  \"result_driven_themes\": [],\n  \"queries\": [\n    {\n      \"query\": \"multivariate split conformal rectangular prediction regions maximum standardized absolute residual score\",\n      \"category\": \"baseline_comparator\",\n      \"rationale\": \"For target multivariate-rectangular-comparators in Prediction Regions and Evaluation Targets, Compared region constructions (paper.tex:85), seek direct support for joint maximum-residual calibration of vector-response rectangles. Standardized-score formulations are relevant family context, but the draft's Simplex max comparator uses unscaled absolute residuals; a source must support the unit-scale specialization before being used for that construction. Existing general split-conformal and adaptive scalar-interval references do not by themselves establish this specific comparator attribution. Reuse an existing bibliography entry if its actual content supplies the needed support; do not imply that the exact simplex-intersected implementation originated in the source.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"query\": \"multivariate conformal prediction simultaneous prediction intervals coordinatewise Bonferroni joint maximum residual calibration\",\n      \"category\": \"baseline_comparator\",\n      \"rationale\": \"For the same paragraph at paper.tex:85, especially the anchor 'The latter is a simultaneous-coordinate baseline', seek direct context for alpha/K coordinatewise conformal intervals and their distinction from a jointly calibrated maximum-residual region. Prefer a source explicitly discussing both constructions; complementary sources may support them separately. This query targets multiplicity and simultaneous vector coverage rather than another generic adaptive-interval search. Cite only the constructions verified in the source, without asserting comparative efficiency, a new validity theorem, or publication of the exact compositional implementation. One source covering both queries is sufficient.\"\n    }\n  ],\n  \"forbidden_query_patterns\": [\n    \"Any attempt to identify, infer, search for, or retrieve the prohibited paper, its authors, title, results, mirrors, summaries, code, or pages citing, discussing, or reproducing it\",\n    \"Broad joint-topic discovery searches such as conformal prediction compositional outputs evaluation or conformal uncertainty simplex benchmark that could directly target the prohibited study; search separate foundational neighborhoods instead\",\n    \"Citation-network expansion, cited-by searches, related-paper recommendations, or unrestricted survey crawling around potentially prohibited material\",\n    \"Queries treating classification labels, softmax probabilities, latent categorical probabilities, CIFAR-10, image classifiers, or text/topic datasets as evaluated responses or benchmarks\",\n    \"Queries presenting Hellinger, Euclidean-versus-ILR, additive-logratio, Mondrian, boundary-face mass, structural zeros, neural architectures, or architecture transfer as implemented comparisons\",\n    \"Queries presenting weighted conformal, jackknife+, conformalized quantile regression, credal sets, clustered conformal, or multivalid prediction as evaluated methods\",\n    \"Generic optimization, benchmarking, scientific-method, paper-writing, campaign chronology, or internal scoring and acceptance terminology without a specific grounded scientific purpose\",\n    \"Queries asserting distribution-free conditional validity, shift robustness, universal efficiency superiority, or a causal selector improvement not established by the recorded evidence\",\n    \"New searches for already-supported general calibration, adaptive scaling, compositional geometry, conditional or shifted validity, statistical ingredients, or benchmark context; the only authorized enrichment target is the existing multivariate comparator paragraph\"\n  ],\n  \"failure_reason\": \"\"\n}\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
{"type":"item.started","item":{"id":"item_8","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/sh -lc \"sed -n '68,94p' paper.tex; cat .codex/skills/validate-paper/SKILL.md\"","aggregated_output":"","exit_code":null,"status":"in_progress"}}
{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_8","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/sh -lc \"sed -n '68,94p' paper.tex; cat .codex/skills/validate-paper/SKILL.md\"","aggregated_output":"For each fitted instance, empirical coverage $\\widehat c_\\alpha$ is the fraction of test responses inside the region. Reliability error is $e_\\alpha=|\\widehat c_\\alpha-(1-\\alpha)|$, treating overcoverage and undercoverage symmetrically. Efficiency is the normalized uniform-simplex reference mass\n\\begin{equation}\n M_\\alpha=\\mathbb E_{X_{\\rm test}}\\Pr_{V\\sim\\operatorname{Dirichlet}(\\mathbf1)}\n \\{V\\in C_\\alpha(X_{\\rm test})\\}.\n \\label{eq:mass}\n\\end{equation}\nThe implementation estimates this probability with shared probes and test contexts, not by integrating ILR-box volume. Thus $M_\\alpha\\in[0,1]$ is dimension-normalized simplex volume, not probability content under the response law. We use ``mass'' to keep the reference distribution explicit. Small mass alone does not establish informative calibrated prediction. % @evidence[[source_evidence/evaluate_final.py#generate_arrays]]\n\nThe supplied utility combines the two empirical quantities:\n\\begin{equation}\n U_\\alpha=(1-\\widehat M_\\alpha)\n \\max\\left\\{0,1-\\frac{e_\\alpha}{1-\\alpha}\\right\\}.\n \\label{eq:utility}\n\\end{equation}\nIt rewards small regions and penalizes coverage error but is not a coverage constraint. A method can improve $U_\\alpha$ by trading mass against error; its ordering need not agree with either component. We therefore report all components and treat utility as a supplied descriptive summary, not a proof of scientific superiority. Overall utility averages conditions and nominal levels equally. IID and shifted errors are averaged separately, after taking absolute deviations within each seed, condition, and level. Taking the absolute value after averaging coverage would produce a different estimand. % @evidence[[source_evidence/evaluate_final.py#aggregate]]\n\n\\subsection{Compared region constructions}\n\\textbf{Scaled ILR} uses Eq.~\\eqref{eq:score} with the fitted scales described below. \\textbf{Unscaled ILR} retains exactly the same center and basis but sets every scale to one; it removes the entire scaling package, not only the local correction. \\textbf{Simplex max} maps the common center through $h$ and calibrates $\\max_k|y_k-h_k(\\widehat\\mu(x))|$. \\textbf{Bonferroni} calibrates separate absolute residuals in each simplex coordinate at miscoverage $\\alpha/K$ and intersects the resulting intervals with the simplex. The latter is a simultaneous-coordinate baseline, not a collection of independently claimed marginal intervals. All thresholds use their own appropriate calibration residuals from the same calibration split. % @evidence[[source_evidence/evaluate_final.py#method_records]]\n\nThese comparisons hold center predictions fixed while changing the residual geometry or scaling. The unscaled comparison can support conclusions about the complete scaling construction within this evaluation. It cannot identify the effect of local smoothing, cross-fitting, shrinkage, or the selector individually. The simplex comparison additionally changes coordinates, so any observed contrast is a comparison between region constructions rather than a causal estimate for one isolated component.\n\n\\section{Method: Training-Only Scale Adaptation}\n\\label{sec:method}\n\\subsection{Rationale and base fits}\nThe design hypothesis is that feature-dependent residual variability makes constant-width ILR regions inefficient, and that a bounded, shrunk correction may allocate widths more usefully. The counter-hypothesis is equally important: a correction selected using a transformed-space size objective may not reduce simplex mass, especially under extrapolation. The study evaluates these possibilities through paired region comparisons, not through a claim of optimal scale estimation.\n\nThe design vector is $\\phi(x)=(1,x,x^{\\odot2},\\sin x,x_0x_1)$, with coordinatewise squares and sine terms. Ridge regression of $z(Y)$ on $\\phi(X)$ uses penalty 1. A second ridge fit, with penalty 10, predicts $\\log\\max(|z_j(Y)-\\widehat\\mu_j(X)|,0.02)$ from the same design. These base-scale residuals are in-sample; cross-fitting is used for the subsequent moment correction, not retroactively for the base log-residual fit. The intercept penalty is $10^{-8}$. Predicted centers are clipped to $[-100,100]$ and base scales to $[10^{-4},100]$. These are numerical bounds, not distributional assumptions. % @evidence[[source_evidence/selected_model.py#_features]]\n---\nname: validate-paper\ndescription: Validate grounded paper artifacts and compile LaTeX PDF with strict fail-fast checks.\n---\n\n# What the skill is about\nRun final quality gate for the generated paper.\n\n# When to use the skill\n- After `paper.tex`, `references.bib`, and `claims_grounding.json` are written.\n- Before declaring final paper success.\n\n# What to do\n1. Validate sections and citation keys:\n```bash\npython scripts/validate_tex_refs.py \\\n  --tex paper.tex \\\n  --bib references.bib\n```\nRequire a substantive embedded Supplementary Information section after the bibliography. It must\nuse `\\\\appendix` and `\\\\section{Supplementary Information}`, contain grounded visible content plus\nat least one subsection, table, or figure, and contain no template placeholder text.\n2. Validate claim mappings, bibliography/provenance consistency, and every `@evidence[[...]]` link in the `scientific-evidence/` bundle:\n```bash\npython ../write-paper/scripts/validate_claims.py \\\n  --paper-data paper_data.json \\\n  --references references_provenance.json \\\n  --bib references.bib \\\n  --claims claims_grounding.json \\\n  --paper-dir .\n```\nThis delegates to the `scientific-evidence` skill verifier, which classifies every `@evidence[[...]]` link as `resolved`, `anchored`, or `dangling`. Any `dangling` link (or an `@evidence[[...]]` token with no `scientific-evidence/` bundle present) is a hard error and must be sent back to the paper-writing agent for repair.\n3. Validate figure manifest and figure readability budget when `figure_manifest.json` is present or `paper_strategy.json` requires figures. The canonical schema is `templates/figure_manifest.schema.json`.\n```bash\npython .claude/skills/validate-paper/scripts/validate_figures.py \\\n  --paper-dir .\n```\n4. Compile:\n```bash\nbash scripts/compile_latex.sh paper.tex\n```\nThe compile script also validates that Supplementary Information is visibly rendered on a page\nafter References with substantive content. Source-only structure checks are not sufficient for\nconditional or literal LaTeX constructs.\nIt records and validates every LaTeX input, restricts BibTeX to the canonical `references.bib`,\nand removes inherited Kpathsea path overrides before compiling.\n5. Require `paper.pdf` to exist.\n6. When `paper_data.json.paper_profile == \"standalone\"`, also fail on visible paper language that leaks pipeline terms such as `eligible`, `correct and eligible`, `correctness rate`, `worker_01`, `worker_02`, `combined_score`, or similar internal wording.\n7. When the selected format or venue guidance requires real author metadata, also fail on anonymous or generic placeholder author blocks and emit soft warnings when title/abstract/introduction/conclusion lack venue-relevant framing or when the discussion drifts into unrelated application narratives.\n8. Fail hard on any missing grounding link, missing section, missing/empty/misordered Supplementary Information, unresolved citation, bibliography/provenance mismatch, figure-manifest mismatch, density-budget violation, page-limit violation for formats with enforced budgets, standalone-language violation, author-block violation required by format/venue guidance, dangling `@evidence[[...]]` link, or compile error.\n9. Treat the parent orchestrator's validation report as authoritative during repair rounds. Last-body-page fill is rendered geometric vertical occupancy computed from text and graphics bounds, not character count or word density. Do not claim that page validation passed using a character-count proxy. If the canonical validator is unavailable in the agent workspace, make the targeted repair and return control so the parent can compile and revalidate it.\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
