{"type":"item.started","item":{"id":"item_18","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/sh -lc \"python - <<'PY'\nfrom pathlib import Path\nfrom bs4 import BeautifulSoup\npage=BeautifulSoup(Path('/tmp/comparator_2512.15383.html').read_text(),'html.parser')\nfor section in page.select('.ltx_bibliography'): section.decompose()\nfor heading in page.select('h1,h2,h3'): print(heading.get_text(' ',strip=True))\nfor paragraph in page.select('.ltx_para'):\n text=paragraph.get_text(' ',strip=True)\n if any(term in text.lower() for term in ['bonferroni','maximum','hyperrectangle','hyper-rectangle']):\n  if len(text)<9000: print('\\\\nID:',paragraph.get('id'),'TEXT:',text)\nPY\"","aggregated_output":"","exit_code":null,"status":"in_progress"}}
{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_18","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/sh -lc \"python - <<'PY'\nfrom pathlib import Path\nfrom bs4 import BeautifulSoup\npage=BeautifulSoup(Path('/tmp/comparator_2512.15383.html').read_text(),'html.parser')\nfor section in page.select('.ltx_bibliography'): section.decompose()\nfor heading in page.select('h1,h2,h3'): print(heading.get_text(' ',strip=True))\nfor paragraph in page.select('.ltx_para'):\n text=paragraph.get_text(' ',strip=True)\n if any(term in text.lower() for term in ['bonferroni','maximum','hyperrectangle','hyper-rectangle']):\n  if len(text)<9000: print('\\\\nID:',paragraph.get('id'),'TEXT:',text)\nPY\"","aggregated_output":"Interpretable Multivariate Conformal Prediction with Fast Transductive Standardization\n1 Introduction\n1.1 Motivation and Preview of Contributions\n1.2 Related Works\n1.3 Outline\n2 Preliminaries\n2.1 Notation\n2.2 Setup and Data Assumptions\n2.3 Computing Residuals using a Pre-Trained Model\n2.4 Calibrating Prediction Sets with Marginal Coverage\n2.5 An Ideal Population Oracle\n2.6 Simple but Unsatisfactory Practical Approaches\n3 Methodology\n3.1 A Transductive Rescaling Oracle and High-Level Method Blueprint\n3.2 Explicit Form of the Link Functions\n3.3 A Conservative Global-Worst-Case Approach\n3.4 A Tighter Local-Worst-Case Method\n4 Numerical Experiments\n4.1 Methods and Metrics\n4.2 Experiments with Simulated Data\n4.3 Experiments with Real Data\n5 Discussion and Future Work\nSoftware Availability\nAppendix A Additional Algorithmic Details\nA.1 Implementation of Alternative Methods\nA.2 Auxiliary Algorithms\nA.3 Computational Cost Analysis\nAppendix B Mathematical Proofs\nB.1 Auxiliary Lemmas\nB.2 Proofs of Main Results\nAppendix C Additional Numerical Results\nC.1 Additional Results from the Experiments of Section 4\nC.2 Comparison of Alternative Implementations of TSCP\nC.3 Robustness to Heavy-Tailed Data\nInstructions for reporting errors\n\nID: S1.SS1.p4 TEXT: Existing methods achieve only a subset of these desiderata. The simplest approach to obtain hyper-rectangular prediction sets is to apply univariate conformal prediction to each output and then take the Cartesian product of the resulting intervals ( Neeven and Smirnov, 2018 ) ; however, a conservative Bonferroni adjustment to the marginal miscoverage levels is needed to guarantee joint coverage; see Algorithm S1 in Appendix A . This strategy is very easy to implement, but the Bonferroni correction is often too conservative to be practically satisfactory, as it adopts a worst-case view of the dependence structure across output dimensions.\nBy contrast, we aim to achieve valid coverage with joint prediction intervals that are as tight as possible; see Figure 1 for a sketch of this goal.\n\nID: S1.SS2.p3 TEXT: To the best of our knowledge, the idea of directly addressing heterogeneity across output dimensions via marginal residual rescaling remains relatively under-explored. Baheri and Amiri Shahbazi (2025) pursue a related idea, but rely on a conservative Bonferroni-type correction. The approach of Sampson and Chan (2024) is more closely related to ours, but requires an additional independent dataset; avoiding this requirement is the main technical challenge addressed in our paper. As we show empirically, the method of Baheri and Amiri Shahbazi (2025) is often considerably less efficient than ours—particularly in small-sample regimes—resulting in less stable and less informative prediction sets.\n\nID: S2.SS4.p2 TEXT: A naive way to achieve this is to set W j = Q ^ 1 − α / d ​ ( E j 1 , … , E j n ) W_{j}=\\hat{Q}_{1-\\alpha/d}(E^{1}_{j},\\ldots,E^{n}_{j}) , which is equivalent to applying univariate conformal prediction separately across all d d outcome dimensions, with a Bonferroni correction ( α / d \\alpha/d replacing α \\alpha ).\nAs demonstrated empirically in Appendix C , this typically leads to very conservative prediction sets because it takes an overly pessimistic worst-case view of possible dependencies of the prediction tasks across outcome dimensions.\n\nID: S2.SS4.p6 TEXT: In this paper, we propose a conceptually simpler solution that consists of standardizing the residuals prior to reducing their dimension by taking the maximum across coordinates, similar to the ℓ ∞ \\ell_{\\infty} norm. This leads to rectangular prediction sets that are uniformly tight across all output dimensions, without requiring additional training data or expensive computations.\nDespite the apparent simplicity of this idea, however, achieving all desiderata while maintaining guaranteed finite-sample coverage involves significant technical challenges, which we overcome as gradually explained below.\n\nID: S2.SS5.p3 TEXT: Although practically unfeasible, this oracle prediction set satisfies all four of our desiderata: (i) it guarantees finite-sample marginal coverage, as per Theorem 1 ; (ii) it tends to be uniformly tight across all output dimensions, because it operates with residuals whose components are all on the same scale; (iii) it is fast to compute, at least for standard choices of the residual function E E ; (iv) it is an easy-to-interpret hyper-rectangle, at least for standard choices of E E .\n\nID: S3.SS4.SSS4.p4 TEXT: The last remaining step to make our method practical is finding an efficient algorithm for computing the boundaries ℒ j \\mathcal{L}_{j} for all j ∈ [ d ] j\\in[d] , without requiring enumeration of all local prediction sets.\nSuch a shortcut is made possible by the following result, which simplifies the definition of ℒ j \\mathcal{L}_{j} in ( 27 ) from a maximum over ( n + 1 ) d (n+1)^{d} distinct values to a maximum over an explicit, narrowed-down list of only n + 1 n+1 relevant candidates.\nBefore stating this result, it is helpful to introduce some notation.\n\nID: A2.SS2.p5 TEXT: Fix any j ∈ [ d ] j\\in[d] and let g ⁡ ( t j , z j ) := t j − μ ^ j ​ ( z j ) σ ^ j ​ ( z j ) . g(t_{j},z_{j}):=\\frac{t_{j}-\\hat{\\mu}_{j}(z_{j})}{\\hat{\\sigma}_{j}(z_{j})}. Finding the supremum of this g g over z j ≥ 0 z_{j}\\geq 0 is a simple univariate optimization problem. Expanding g g in full, we get g ⁡ ( t j , z j ) = t j − μ ^ j ​ ( z j ) σ ^ j ​ ( z j ) = t j − 1 n + 1 ​ ( z j + n ​ μ ^ j ) 1 n + 1 ​ ( z j − μ ^ j ) 2 + σ ^ j 2 , g(t_{j},z_{j})=\\frac{t_{j}-\\hat{\\mu}_{j}(z_{j})}{\\hat{\\sigma}_{j}(z_{j})}=\\frac{t_{j}-\\frac{1}{n+1}(z_{j}+n\\hat{\\mu}_{j})}{\\sqrt{\\frac{1}{n+1}(z_{j}-\\hat{\\mu}_{j})^{2}+\\hat{\\sigma}_{j}^{2}}}, where the last equality follows from Lemma S10 .\nDifferentiating g g with respect to z j z_{j} yields g z j ​ ( t j , z j ) = − 1 n + 1 ​ 1 n + 1 ​ ( z j − μ ^ j ) 2 + σ ^ j 2 − ( t j − 1 n + 1 ​ ( z j + n ​ μ ^ j ) ) ​ 1 n + 1 ​ ( z j − μ ^ j ) 1 n + 1 ​ ( z j − μ ^ j ) 2 + σ ^ j 2 1 n + 1 ​ ( z j − μ ^ j ) 2 + σ ^ j 2 ​ 2 . g_{z_{j}}(t_{j},z_{j})=\\frac{-\\tfrac{1}{n+1}\\sqrt{\\frac{1}{n+1}(z_{j}-\\hat{\\mu}_{j})^{2}+\\hat{\\sigma}_{j}^{2}}-\\big(t_{j}-\\tfrac{1}{n+1}(z_{j}+n\\hat{\\mu}_{j})\\big)\\tfrac{\\tfrac{1}{n+1}(z_{j}-\\hat{\\mu}_{j})}{\\sqrt{\\frac{1}{n+1}(z_{j}-\\hat{\\mu}_{j})^{2}+\\hat{\\sigma}_{j}^{2}}}}{\\frac{1}{n+1}(z_{j}-\\hat{\\mu}_{j})^{2}+\\hat{\\sigma}_{j}^{2}2}. Solving g z j ​ ( t j , z j ∗ ) = 0 g_{z_{j}}(t_{j},z^{*}_{j})=0 with respect to z j ∗ z^{*}_{j} gives: σ ^ j 2 + ( t j − μ ^ j ) ​ ( z j ∗ − μ ^ j ) = 0 ⟹ z j ∗ = μ ^ j − σ ^ j 2 t j − μ ^ j . \\hat{\\sigma}_{j}^{2}+(t_{j}-\\hat{\\mu}_{j})(z^{*}_{j}-\\hat{\\mu}_{j})=0\\quad\\Longrightarrow\\quad z^{\\ast}_{j}=\\hat{\\mu}_{j}-\\frac{\\hat{\\sigma}_{j}^{2}}{t_{j}-\\hat{\\mu}_{j}}. There are three cases here for any fixed t j ≥ 0 t_{j}\\geq 0 : • Case 1: if g ⁡ ( t j , z j ∗ ) g(t_{j},z^{\\ast}_{j}) is indeed the global maximum and z j ∗ > 0 z^{\\ast}_{j}>0 , then we can just take sup z j ≥ 0 g ⁡ ( t j , z j ) = g ⁡ ( t j , z j ∗ ) . \\sup_{z_{j}\\geq 0}g(t_{j},z_{j})=g(t_{j},z^{\\ast}_{j}). • Case 2: if g ⁡ ( t j , z j ∗ ) g(t_{j},z^{\\ast}_{j}) is not the global maximum or z j ∗