right]\\neq\\emptyset,\\quad\\forall j\\in[d]. Fix any t ∈ ℝ + d t\\in\\mathbb{R}^{d}_{+} . Similar to the proof of Lemma 4 , we have to solve a univariate optimization problem. We first notice that for any j ∈ [ d ] j\\in[d] , sup z ∈ R h t j σ ^ j ​ ( z j ) = sup z j ∈ ℐ j h t j σ ^ j ​ ( z j ) = t j inf z j ∈ ℐ j h σ ^ j ​ ( z j ) . \\sup_{z\\in R^{h}}\\frac{t_{j}}{\\hat{\\sigma}_{j}(z_{j})}=\\sup_{z_{j}\\in\\mathcal{I}_{j}^{h}}\\frac{t_{j}}{\\hat{\\sigma}_{j}(z_{j})}=\\frac{t_{j}}{\\inf_{z_{j}\\in\\mathcal{I}_{j}^{h}}\\hat{\\sigma}_{j}(z_{j})}. By Lemma S10 , we know that σ ^ j ​ ( z j ) = 1 n + 1 ​ ( z j − μ ^ j ) 2 + σ ^ j 2 , \\hat{\\sigma}_{j}(z_{j})=\\sqrt{\\frac{1}{n+1}(z_{j}-\\hat{\\mu}_{j})^{2}+\\hat{\\sigma}_{j}^{2}}, so clearly σ ^ j ​ ( z j ) \\hat{\\sigma}_{j}(z_{j}) is minimized at z j ∗ = argmin z j ∈ ℐ j h | z j − μ ^ j | = { μ ^ j , if ​ μ ^ j ∈ ℐ j h , E ( h j − 1 ) , if ​ μ ^ j < E ( h j − 1 ) , U j h j , if ​ μ ^ j > U j h j . z^{*}_{j}=\\operatorname*{argmin}_{z_{j}\\in\\mathcal{I}_{j}^{h}}|z_{j}-\\hat{\\mu}_{j}|=\\begin{cases}\\hat{\\mu}_{j},&\\text{ if }\\hat{\\mu}_{j}\\in\\mathcal{I}_{j}^{h},\\\\\nE^{(h_{j}-1)},&\\text{ if }\\hat{\\mu}_{j}<E^{(h_{j}-1)},\\\\\nU^{h_{j}}_{j},&\\text{ if }\\hat{\\mu}_{j}>U^{h_{j}}_{j}.\\end{cases} Hence, r j ​ ( h j ) = σ ^ j ​ ( z j ∗ ) r_{j}(h_{j})=\\hat{\\sigma}_{j}(z^{*}_{j}) is exactly the infimum proposed.\nFor the second part, denote g ⁡ ( z j ) := μ ^ j ​ ( z j ) σ ^ j ​ ( z j ) = 1 n + 1 ​ ( z j + n ​ μ ^ j ) 1 n + 1 ​ ( z j − μ ^ j ) 2 + σ ^ j 2 = 1 n + 1 ​ ( z j − μ ^ j ) + μ ^ j 1 n + 1 ​ ( z j − μ ^ j ) 2 + σ ^ j 2 , g(z_{j}):=\\frac{\\hat{\\mu}_{j}(z_{j})}{\\hat{\\sigma}_{j}(z_{j})}=\\frac{\\tfrac{1}{n+1}(z_{j}+n\\hat{\\mu}_{j})}{\\sqrt{\\tfrac{1}{n+1}(z_{j}-\\hat{\\mu}_{j})^{2}+\\hat{\\sigma}_{j}^{2}}}=\\frac{\\tfrac{1}{n+1}(z_{j}-\\hat{\\mu}_{j})+\\hat{\\mu}_{j}}{\\sqrt{\\tfrac{1}{n+1}(z_{j}-\\hat{\\mu}_{j})^{2}+\\hat{\\sigma}_{j}^{2}}}, then we want to find inf z ∈ ℰ gwc g ⁡ ( z j ) = inf z j