{"type":"item.started","item":{"id":"item_21","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/sh -lc \"python - <<'PY'\nfrom pathlib import Path\nfrom bs4 import BeautifulSoup\npage=BeautifulSoup(Path('/tmp/comparator_2512.15383.html').read_text(),'html.parser')\nfor section_id in ['S2.SS3','S2.SS4','S2.SS6','A1.SS1']:\n section=page.find(id=section_id)\n print('\\\\nSECTION',section_id)\n if section: print(section.get_text(' ',strip=True)[:18000])\n else:\n  print([(heading.get('id'),heading.get_text(' ',strip=True)) for heading in page.select('h2') if 'Alternative' in heading.get_text()])\nprint('authors',[(meta.get('name'),meta.get('content')) for meta in page.select('meta[name=\\\"citation_author\\\"], meta[name=\\\"citation_date\\\"],meta[name=\\\"citation_title\\\"]')])\nPY\"","aggregated_output":"","exit_code":null,"status":"in_progress"}}
{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_21","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/sh -lc \"python - <<'PY'\nfrom pathlib import Path\nfrom bs4 import BeautifulSoup\npage=BeautifulSoup(Path('/tmp/comparator_2512.15383.html').read_text(),'html.parser')\nfor section_id in ['S2.SS3','S2.SS4','S2.SS6','A1.SS1']:\n section=page.find(id=section_id)\n print('\\\\nSECTION',section_id)\n if section: print(section.get_text(' ',strip=True)[:18000])\n else:\n  print([(heading.get('id'),heading.get_text(' ',strip=True)) for heading in page.select('h2') if 'Alternative' in heading.get_text()])\nprint('authors',[(meta.get('name'),meta.get('content')) for meta in page.select('meta[name=\\\"citation_author\\\"], meta[name=\\\"citation_date\\\"],meta[name=\\\"citation_title\\\"]')])\nPY\"","aggregated_output":"\nSECTION S2.SS3\n2.3 Computing Residuals using a Pre-Trained Model We assume access to a pre-trained joint predictive model f ^ = ( f ^ 1 , … , f ^ d ) \\hat{f}=(\\hat{f}_{1},\\ldots,\\hat{f}_{d}) , fitted on data independent of both the calibration set 𝒟 cal \\mathcal{D}_{\\mathrm{cal}} and the test point.\nThis model is treated as a “black-box” and could be anything; the only requirement for f ^ \\hat{f} is that it must provide us with a coordinate-wise generalized residual function E : 𝒳 × ℝ d → ℝ d , E:\\mathcal{X}\\times\\mathbb{R}^{d}\\to\\mathbb{R}^{d}, which aims to quantify the model’s prediction error along each output dimension.\nApplying this function to a data point ( X , Y ) (X,Y) produces the vector E ⁡ ( X , Y ) = ( E 1 ​ ( X , Y ) , … , E d ​ ( X , Y ) ) E(X,Y)=\\bigl(E_{1}(X,Y),\\ldots,E_{d}(X,Y)\\bigr) . A classical example for models f ^ j \\hat{f}_{j} that estimate the conditional mean of Y j Y_{j} given X X ( Lei et al., 2018 ) is E j ​ ( X , Y ) = | Y j − f ^ j ​ ( X ) | E_{j}(X,Y)=\\lvert Y_{j}-\\hat{f}_{j}(X)\\rvert .\nAlternatively, for models that estimate conditional quantiles, as in conformalized quantile regression ( Romano et al., 2020b ) , a natural choice is E j ​ ( X , Y ) = max ⁡ { f ^ j lo ​ ( X ) − Y j , Y j − f ^ j hi ​ ( X ) } , \\displaystyle E_{j}(X,Y)=\\max\\bigl\\{\\hat{f}_{j}^{\\mathrm{lo}}(X)-Y_{j},\\;Y_{j}-\\hat{f}_{j}^{\\mathrm{hi}}(X)\\bigr\\}, where f ^ j lo ​ ( X ) \\hat{f}_{j}^{\\mathrm{lo}}(X) and f ^ j hi ​ ( X ) \\hat{f}_{j}^{\\mathrm{hi}}(X) denote the estimated lower and upper conditional quantiles of Y j Y_{j} given X X , for example at levels α / 2 \\alpha/2 and 1 − α / 2 1-\\alpha/2 . Intuitively, this choice of E E measures the signed distance of Y j Y_{j} to the nearest boundary of the interval [ f ^ j lo ​ ( X ) , f ^ j hi ​ ( X ) ] [\\hat{f}_{j}^{\\mathrm{lo}}(X),\\hat{f}_{j}^{\\mathrm{hi}}(X)] .\nPositive values indicate that Y j Y_{j} falls outside the interval, while negative values indicate that it lies inside. Without much loss of generality, we assume throughout the paper that the residuals are almost surely distinct and non-negative —which implies E j ​ ( X , Y ) > 0 E_{j}(X,Y)>0 for all j j —since this simplifies the development of our method. Moreover, we also assume these residuals have finite first and second moments at the population level. Assumption 1 . For all j ∈ [ d ] j\\in[d] , the distribution of E j ​ ( X , Y ) E_{j}(X,Y) has no point masses and satisfies: E j ​ ( X , Y ) ≥ 0 E_{j}(X,Y)\\geq 0 almost surely, 𝔼 ⁡ [ E j ​ ( X , Y ) ] < ∞ \\mathbb{E}[E_{j}(X,Y)]<\\infty , and 0 < Var ⁡ [ E j ​ ( X , Y ) ] < ∞ 0<\\operatorname{Var}[E_{j}(X,Y)]<\\infty . The assumption of no point masses is standard in conformal inference and practically without loss of generality, since any ties can always be broken by introducing a small amount of independent random noise. The bounded second moments are also immediately satisfied by any residuals whose distribution has finite support, which is common in practice.\nThe non-negativity assumption is satisfied by absolute residuals and many other standard choices, although it does not hold for the quantile-based residuals reviewed above. Nonetheless, there are at least two simple ways to adapt conformalized quantile regression to this assumption.\nOne is to simply truncate negative residuals at zero, which tends to make the corresponding conformal prediction sets more conservative ( Romano et al., 2020b ) .\nAlternatively, provided that the residuals are almost-surely bounded, as it is often the case in practice, non-negativity can be enforced by applying a sufficiently large constant shift to all calibration and test residuals—a transformation that can then be easily inverted to keep the resulting prediction sets invariant.\nThis idea will become clearer after we review a general recipe for translating residuals into conformal prediction sets.\n\nSECTION S2.SS4\n2.4 Calibrating Prediction Sets with Marginal Coverage After evaluating the residuals E i = E ⁡ ( X i , Y i ) E^{i}=E(X^{i},Y^{i}) for all calibration points indexed by i ∈ [ n ] i\\in[n] , a prediction set for Y n + 1 Y_{n+1} based on X n + 1 X_{n+1} can be assembled by including all possible values of y ∈ ℝ d y\\in\\mathbb{R}^{d} whose residuals ( E 1 ​ ( X n + 1 , y ) , … , E d ​ ( X n + 1 , y ) ) (E_{1}(X^{n+1},y),\\ldots,E_{d}(X^{n+1},y)) are sufficiently small: C ^ ( X n + 1 ) = { y ∈ ℝ d : 0 ≤ E j ( X n + 1 , y ) ≤ W j , ∀ j ∈ [ d ] } , \\hat{C}(X^{n+1})=\\left\\{y\\in\\mathbb{R}^{d}:0\\leq E_{j}(X^{n+1},y)\\leq W_{j},\\quad\\forall j\\in[d]\\right\\}, (3) where W 1 , … , W d W_{1},\\ldots,W_{d} are suitable (data-dependent) thresholds.\nTo ensure that C ^ ​ ( X n + 1 ) \\hat{C}(X^{n+1}) satisfies marginal coverage at level 1 − α 1-\\alpha , as defined in ( 1 ), the thresholds must be chosen such that ℙ [ E j ( X n + 1 , Y n + 1 ) ≤ W j , ∀ j ∈ [ d ] ] ≥ 1 − α . \\displaystyle\\mathbb{P}\\left[E_{j}(X^{n+1},Y^{n+1})\\leq W_{j},\\;\\forall j\\in[d]\\right]\\geq 1-\\alpha. A naive way to achieve this is to set W j = Q ^ 1 − α / d ​ ( E j 1 , … , E j n ) W_{j}=\\hat{Q}_{1-\\alpha/d}(E^{1}_{j},\\ldots,E^{n}_{j}) , which is equivalent to applying univariate conformal prediction separately across all d d outcome dimensions, with a Bonferroni correction ( α / d \\alpha/d replacing α \\alpha ).\nAs demonstrated empirically in Appendix C , this typically leads to very conservative prediction sets because it takes an overly pessimistic worst-case view of possible dependencies of the prediction tasks across outcome dimensions. In this paper, we pursue a more efficient approach, which starts by computing scalar non-conformity scores S i = Φ ⁡ ( E i ) ∈ ℝ S^{i}=\\Phi(E^{i})\\in\\mathbb{R} for all i ∈ [ n ] i\\in[n] using a dimension-reduction function Φ : ℝ + d ↦ ℝ \\Phi:\\mathbb{R}^{d}_{+}\\mapsto\\mathbb{R} , whose form will be discussed below.\nThen, the prediction set is constructed as: C ^ ​ ( X n + 1 ) = { y ∈ ℝ d : Φ ⁡ ( E ⁡ ( X n + 1 , y ) ) ≤ Q ^ 1 − α ​ ( S 1 , … , S n ) } . \\hat{C}(X^{n+1})=\\left\\{y\\in\\mathbb{R}^{d}:\\Phi(E(X^{n+1},y))\\leq\\hat{Q}_{1-\\alpha}(S^{1},\\ldots,S^{n})\\right\\}. (4) Theorem 1 ( Vovk et al. (2005) ) . Assume ( E 1 , … , E n + 1 ) (E^{1},\\ldots,E^{n+1}) are exchangeable, with E i = E ⁡ ( X i , Y i ) E^{i}=E(X^{i},Y^{i}) for all i ∈ [ n + 1 ] i\\in[n+1] .\nLet Φ : ℝ + d ↦ ℝ \\Phi:\\mathbb{R}^{d}_{+}\\mapsto\\mathbb{R} be any fixed function, independent of the data.\nThen, C ^ ​ ( X n + 1 ) \\hat{C}(X^{n+1}) defined in ( 4 ) satisfies ℙ [ Y n + 1 ∈ C ^ ( X n + 1 ) ] ≥ 1 − α \\mathbb{P}[Y^{n+1}\\in\\hat{C}(X^{n+1})]\\geq 1-\\alpha .\nMoreover, if the scores S 1 , … , S n S^{1},\\ldots,S^{n} given by S i = Φ ⁡ ( E i ) S^{i}=\\Phi(E^{i}) are almost-surely distinct, ℙ [ Y n + 1 ∈ C ^ ( X n + 1 ) ] ≤ 1 − α + 1 n + 1 \\mathbb{P}[Y^{n+1}\\in\\hat{C}(X^{n+1})]\\leq 1-\\alpha+\\frac{1}{n+1} .\nThe same results hold with Φ \\Phi random, if ( S 1 , … , S n + 1 ) (S^{1},\\ldots,S^{n+1}) are exchangeable conditional on it. Therefore, under Assumption 1 , Theorem 1 guarantees the prediction sets defined in ( 4 ) achieve marginal coverage tightly from above, as long as the sample size n n is not too small.\nA limitation of Theorem 1 , however, is that it cannot tell us whether the coverage is equally tight across different outcome dimensions.\nIn fact, depending on the data distribution, the fitted model, and the choice of Φ \\Phi , it could happen that C ^ ​ ( X n + 1 ) \\hat{C}(X^{n+1}) achieves the desired marginal coverage while being much wider than necessary along certain directions, thereby providing uncertainty estimates that are potentially much less informative than ideal.\nMoreover, depending on Φ \\Phi , the prediction set C ^ ​ ( X n + 1 ) \\hat{C}(X^{n+1}) may have a difficult-to-interpret shape. Several recent works propose residual transformations based on ℓ p \\ell_{p} -norms, i.e., Φ ( ⋅ ) = ∥ ⋅ ∥ p \\Phi(\\cdot)=\\|\\cdot\\|_{p} for different 1 ≤ p ≤ ∞ 1\\leq p\\leq\\infty ( Johnstone and Ndiaye, 2022 ) . Although conceptually and computationally simple, these can be heavily affected by the nosiest output dimension. A single high-variance dimension may dominate the scalar score and inflate the resulting prediction set uniformly across all coordinates.\nTo obtain tighter prediction sets, recent works have proposed learning a suitable transformation of the residuals prior to applying a norm-based dimension reduction, for example by using optimal transport ( Thurin et al., 2025 ; Klein et al., 2025 ) .\nHowever, these approaches require additional training data as well as expensive computations, and they typically lead to prediction sets with irregular shapes. In this paper, we propose a conceptually simpler solution that consists of standardizing the residuals prior to reducing their dimension by taking the maximum across coordinates, similar to the ℓ ∞ \\ell_{\\infty} norm. This leads to rectangular prediction sets that are uniformly tight across all output dimensions, without requiring additional training data or expensive computations.\nDespite the apparent simplicity of this idea, however, achieving all desiderata while maintaining guaranteed finite-sample coverage involves significant technical challenges, which we overcome as gradually explained below.\n\nSECTION S2.SS6\n2.6 Simple but Unsatisfactory Practical Approaches An intuitive plug-in approximation of the ideal oracle is obtained by replacing the population mean μ ∗ \\mu^{*} and standard deviation σ ∗ \\sigma^{*} parameters in ( 6 ) with their empirical counterparts, μ ^ pi \\hat{\\mu}^{\\mathrm{pi}} and σ ^ pi \\hat{\\sigma}^{\\mathrm{pi}} , evaluated on the calibration data, and then constructing hyper-rectangular prediction sets C ^ pi ​ ( X n + 1 ) \\hat{C}^{\\mathrm{pi}}(X^{n+1}) in the form of ( 3 ) using W j = Q ^ 1 − α pi ⋅ σ ^ j pi + μ ^ j pi W_{j}=\\hat{Q}^{\\mathrm{pi}}_{1-\\alpha}\\cdot\\hat{\\sigma}^{\\mathrm{pi}}_{j}+\\hat{\\mu}^{\\mathrm{pi}}_{j} , where Q ^ 1 − α pi := Q ^ 1 − α ​ ( S pi 1 , … , S pi n ) \\hat{Q}^{\\mathrm{pi}}_{1-\\alpha}:=\\hat{Q}_{1-\\alpha}(S^{1}_{\\mathrm{pi}},\\ldots,S^{n}_{\\mathrm{pi}}) and S pi i := Φ ⁡ ( E i , μ ^ pi , σ ^ pi ) S^{i}_{\\mathrm{pi}}:=\\Phi(E^{i};\\hat{\\mu}^{\\mathrm{pi}},\\hat{\\sigma}^{\\mathrm{pi}}) for all i ∈ [ n ] i\\in[n] . This approach, outlined by Algorithm S3 in Appendix A , tends to perform well in large-samples (as shown empirically in Appendix C ) but lacks finite-sample guarantees.\nIn fact, using a data-dependent transformation Φ \\Phi , which breaks the exchangeability between calibration and test scores in ( S 1 , … , S n + 1 ) (S^{1},\\ldots,S^{n+1}) , violates the key assumption of Theorem 1 . An alternative solution that is also intuitive yet unsatisfactory consists of combining the above plug-in approach with an additional data splitting step that allows one to recover finite-sample guarantees: the location and scale parameter estimates for the transformation function Φ \\Phi , denoted here as μ ^ ds \\hat{\\mu}^{\\mathrm{ds}} and σ ^ ds \\hat{\\sigma}^{\\mathrm{ds}} , are estimated empirically using only a random subset of n 1 < n n_{1}<n observations. The remaining n 2 = n − n 1 n_{2}=n-n_{1} data points are used to evaluate the scores ( S ds 1 , … , S ds n 2 ) (S^{1}_{\\mathrm{ds}},\\ldots,S^{n_{2}}_{\\mathrm{ds}}) and compute Q ^ 1 − α ds := Q ^ 1 − α ​ ( S ds 1 , … , S ds n 2 ) \\hat{Q}^{\\mathrm{ds}}_{1-\\alpha}:=\\hat{Q}_{1-\\alpha}(S^{1}_{\\mathrm{ds}},\\ldots,S^{n_{2}}_{\\mathrm{ds}}) , finally leading to W j = Q ^ 1 − α ds ⋅ σ ^ j ds + μ ^ j ds W_{j}=\\hat{Q}^{\\mathrm{ds}}_{1-\\alpha}\\cdot\\hat{\\sigma}^{\\mathrm{ds}}_{j}+\\hat{\\mu}^{\\mathrm{ds}}_{j} .\nThis approach is outlined by Algorithm S4 in Appendix A .\nWhile it satisfies the key assumption of Theorem 1 , it makes an inefficient use of the data. Since it reduces the effective number of calibration samples roughly by half, it tends to produce more unstable and often wider-than-necessary prediction sets, especially in small-sample regimes. The main contribution of this paper is therefore to overcome this inefficiency. We develop a transductive conformal prediction method that uses a similar standardization idea but avoids wasteful data splitting by reusing the entire calibration data to estimate the scaling parameters, all while retaining finite-sample guarantees and manageable computational costs.\nAchieving this requires resolving several technical challenges, as explained step by step below.\n\nSECTION A1.SS1\nA.1 Implementation of Alternative Methods Algorithm S1 Separate Prediction Intervals with Bonferroni Correction Input: Calibration residuals { E i } i = 1 n \\{E^{i}\\}_{i=1}^{n} ; target miscoverage level α ∈ ( 0 , 1 ) \\alpha\\in(0,1) ; test input X n + 1 X^{n+1} . 1: Compute α ∗ ← α / d \\alpha^{*}\\leftarrow\\alpha/d . 2: for j = 1 j=1 to d d do 3: Compute Q ^ 1 − α ∗ , j ← Q ^ 1 − α ∗ ​ ( E j 1 , … , E j n ) \\hat{Q}_{1-\\alpha^{*},j}\\leftarrow\\hat{Q}_{1-\\alpha^{*}}(E^{1}_{j},\\ldots,E^{n}_{j}) . 4: end for 5: return C ^ Bonf . ( X n + 1 ) ← { y ∈ ℝ d : 0 ≤ E j ( X n + 1 , y ) ≤ Q ^ 1 − α ∗ , j , ∀ j ∈ [ d ] } \\hat{C}^{\\mathrm{Bonf.}}(X^{n+1})\\leftarrow\\left\\{y\\in\\mathbb{R}^{d}:0\\leq E_{j}(X^{n+1},y)\\leq\\hat{Q}_{1-\\alpha^{*},j},\\forall j\\in[d]\\right\\} . Algorithm S2 Prediction Intervals via Dimension Reduction with ℓ ∞ \\ell_{\\infty} -Norm (Unscaled) Input: Calibration residuals { E i } i = 1 n \\{E^{i}\\}_{i=1}^{n} ; target miscoverage level α ∈ ( 0 , 1 ) \\alpha\\in(0,1) ; test input X n + 1 X^{n+1} . 1: Compute S unscaled i ← ‖ E i ‖ ∞ S^{i}_{\\mathrm{unscaled}}\\leftarrow\\|E^{i}\\|_{\\infty} for i = 1 , … , n i=1,\\ldots,n . 2: Compute Q ^ 1 − α unscaled ← Q ^ 1 − α ​ ( S unscaled 1 , … , S unscaled n ) \\hat{Q}^{\\mathrm{unscaled}}_{1-\\alpha}\\leftarrow\\hat{Q}_{1-\\alpha}(S^{1}_{\\mathrm{unscaled}},\\dots,S^{n}_{\\mathrm{unscaled}}) 3: return C ^ unscaled ( X n + 1 ) ← { y ∈ ℝ d : 0 ≤ E j n + 1 ( y ) ≤ Q ^ 1 − α unscaled , ∀ j ∈ [ d ] } \\hat{C}^{\\mathrm{unscaled}}(X^{n+1})\\leftarrow\\left\\{y\\in\\mathbb{R}^{d}:0\\leq E^{n+1}_{j}(y)\\leq\\hat{Q}^{\\mathrm{unscaled}}_{1-\\alpha},\\forall j\\in[d]\\right\\} . Algorithm S3 Heuristic Plug-In Standardization Input: Calibration residuals { E i } i = 1 n \\{E^{i}\\}_{i=1}^{n} ; target miscoverage level α ∈ ( 0 , 1 ) \\alpha\\in(0,1) ; test input X n + 1 X^{n+1} . 1: for j = 1 j=1 to d d do 2: Compute the location and scale parameters μ ^ j pi ← 1 n ​ ∑ i = 1 n E j i , \\displaystyle\\hat{\\mu}^{\\mathrm{pi}}_{j}\\leftarrow\\frac{1}{n}\\sum_{i=1}^{n}E^{i}_{j}, σ ^ j pi ← 1 n − 1 ​ ∑ i = 1 n ( E j i − μ ^ j pi ) 2 . \\displaystyle\\hat{\\sigma}^{\\mathrm{pi}}_{j}\\leftarrow\\sqrt{\\frac{1}{n-1}\\sum_{i=1}^{n}(E^{i}_{j}-\\hat{\\mu}^{\\mathrm{pi}}_{j})^{2}}. 3: end for 4: Compute S pi i ← Φ ⁡ ( E i , μ ^ j pi , σ ^ j pi ) S^{i}_{\\mathrm{pi}}\\leftarrow\\Phi(E^{i};\\hat{\\mu}_{j}^{\\mathrm{pi}},\\hat{\\sigma}_{j}^{\\mathrm{pi}}) for i ∈ [ n ] i\\in[n] , using Φ \\Phi defined in ( 5 ). 5: Compute Q ^ 1 − α pi ← Q ^ 1 − α ​ ( S pi 1 , … , S pi n ) . \\hat{Q}^{\\mathrm{pi}}_{1-\\alpha}\\leftarrow\\hat{Q}_{1-\\alpha}(S^{1}_{\\mathrm{pi}},\\dots,S^{n}_{\\mathrm{pi}}). 6: Compute C ^ pi ( X n + 1 ) ← { y ∈ ℝ d : 0 ≤ E j ( X n + 1 , y ) ≤ Q ^ 1 − α pi ⋅ σ ^ j pi + μ ^ j pi , ∀ j ∈ [ d ] } , \\displaystyle\\hat{C}^{\\mathrm{pi}}(X^{n+1})\\leftarrow\\left\\{y\\in\\mathbb{R}^{d}:0\\leq E_{j}(X^{n+1},y)\\leq\\hat{Q}^{\\mathrm{pi}}_{1-\\alpha}\\cdot\\hat{\\sigma}_{j}^{\\mathrm{pi}}+\\hat{\\mu}_{j}^{\\mathrm{pi}},\\forall j\\in[d]\\right\\}, using either ( 8 ) (for absolute regression residuals) or ( 9 ) (for quantile regression residuals). 7: return Prediction set C ^ pi ​ ( X n + 1 ) \\hat{C}^{\\mathrm{pi}}(X^{n+1}) . Algorithm S4 Standardization with Data Splitting Input: Calibration residuals { E i } i = 1 n \\{E^{i}\\}_{i=1}^{n} ; target miscoverage level α ∈ ( 0 , 1 ) \\alpha\\in(0,1) ; test input X n + 1 X^{n+1} . Output: C ^ ds ​ ( X n + 1 ) \\hat{C}^{\\mathrm{ds}}(X^{n+1}) 1: Randomly split { 1 , … , n } \\{1,\\ldots,n\\} into two disjoint subsets, ℐ 1 \\mathcal{I}_{1} and ℐ 2 \\mathcal{I}_{2} . 2: for j = 1 j=1 to d d do 3: Compute the location and scale parameters μ ^ j ds ← 1 | ℐ 1 | ​ ∑ i ∈ I 1 E j i , \\displaystyle\\hat{\\mu}^{\\mathrm{ds}}_{j}\\leftarrow\\frac{1}{|\\mathcal{I}_{1}|}\\sum_{i\\in I_{1}}E^{i}_{j}, σ ^ j ds ← 1 | ℐ 1 | − 1 ​ ∑ i ∈ I 1 ( E j i − μ ^ j ) 2 . \\displaystyle\\hat{\\sigma}^{\\mathrm{ds}}_{j}\\leftarrow\\sqrt{\\frac{1}{|\\mathcal{I}_{1}|-1}\\sum_{i\\in I_{1}}(E^{i}_{j}-\\hat{\\mu}_{j})^{2}}. 4: end for 5: Compute S ds i ← Φ ⁡ ( E i , μ ^ j ds , σ ^ j ds ) S^{i}_{\\mathrm{ds}}\\leftarrow\\Phi(E^{i};\\hat{\\mu}_{j}^{\\mathrm{ds}},\\hat{\\sigma}_{j}^{\\mathrm{ds}}) for i ∈ ℐ 2 i\\in\\mathcal{I}_{2} , using Φ \\Phi defined in ( 5 ). 6: Compute Q ^ 1 − α ds ← Q ^ 1 − α ​ ( S ds 1 , … , S ds n ) . \\hat{Q}^{\\mathrm{ds}}_{1-\\alpha}\\leftarrow\\hat{Q}_{1-\\alpha}(S^{1}_{\\mathrm{ds}},\\dots,S^{n}_{\\mathrm{ds}}). 7: return C ^ ds ( X n + 1 ) ← { y ∈ ℝ d : 0 ≤ E j ( X n + 1 , y ) ≤ Q ^ 1 − α ds ⋅ σ ^ j ds + μ ^ j ds , ∀ j ∈ [ d ] } \\hat{C}^{\\mathrm{ds}}(X^{n+1})\\leftarrow\\left\\{y\\in\\mathbb{R}^{d}:0\\leq E_{j}(X^{n+1},y)\\leq\\hat{Q}^{\\mathrm{ds}}_{1-\\alpha}\\cdot\\hat{\\sigma}_{j}^{\\mathrm{ds}}+\\hat{\\mu}_{j}^{\\mathrm{ds}},\\forall j\\in[d]\\right\\} .\nauthors []\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
