{"type":"item.started","item":{"id":"item_62","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/sh -lc \"rg -n '25|26|reference|concept|placeholder' REPRODUCIBILITY.md paper.tex figure_manifest.json | head -48; python - <<'PY'\nimport difflib\nfrom pathlib import Path\nprint(''.join(difflib.unified_diff(Path('/tmp/paper_before_enrichment.tex').read_text().splitlines(True),Path('paper.tex').read_text().splitlines(True),fromfile='before/paper.tex',tofile='after/paper.tex')))\nPY\"","aggregated_output":"","exit_code":null,"status":"in_progress"}}
{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_62","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/sh -lc \"rg -n '25|26|reference|concept|placeholder' REPRODUCIBILITY.md paper.tex figure_manifest.json | head -48; python - <<'PY'\nimport difflib\nfrom pathlib import Path\nprint(''.join(difflib.unified_diff(Path('/tmp/paper_before_enrichment.tex').read_text().splitlines(True),Path('paper.tex').read_text().splitlines(True),fromfile='before/paper.tex',tofile='after/paper.tex')))\nPY\"","aggregated_output":"figure_manifest.json:28:      \"generator\": \"generate-concept-figure\",\npaper.tex:2:\\usepackage{iclr2026_conference,times}\npaper.tex:14:Prediction regions for compositional responses must account for both the simplex constraint and variation in predictive uncertainty. We study whether scale-adaptive split-conformal regions in isometric-logratio coordinates improve reliability and efficiency together. A frozen training-only procedure combines ridge centers, local residual-moment scaling, and nested rank-based selection; independent calibration determines the final regions. Across logistic-normal, Dirichlet, and shifted-Dirichlet settings at two dimensions and eight held-out seeds, the results are condition-dependent. Mean absolute coverage error is 0.0136 under IID sampling but 0.1303 under shift. Aggregate utility exceeds the strongest standard comparator by 0.0116, with a descriptive paired 95\\% seed-bootstrap interval of [0.0047, 0.0179], yet mean uniform-simplex region mass is larger: 0.3035 versus 0.2917. Gains concentrate in higher-dimensional IID Dirichlet responses, whereas shifted comparisons favor the simplex-coordinate baseline. These findings support separating observed-response coverage, reference-measure efficiency, and distributional assumptions rather than interpreting an aggregate utility gain as uniform dominance or conditional validity.\npaper.tex:24:We ask: \\emph{how do training-only scale-adaptive logratio regions trade observed-composition coverage against uniform-simplex reference mass, relative to alternatives sharing the same fitted center?} We evaluate one fixed construction rather than claiming a comprehensive comparison of conformal methods. Its scale correction uses local moments of cross-fitted standardized residuals, with a neutral option and nested training-only selection. The final calibration sample is not used to choose that correction. The comparison includes unscaled logratio regions, a joint simplex-coordinate maximum-residual region, and coordinatewise Bonferroni regions.\npaper.tex:28:Our contributions are: (i) an inspectable training-only scale-adaptation specification with independent finite-rank calibration; (ii) a paired synthetic assessment separating coverage, reference mass, and their utility summary; and (iii) a seed-level uncertainty analysis that retains unfavorable condition-specific comparisons and distinguishes scaling effects from untested selector-specific explanations. The scope is strictly positive continuous responses under the stated generators. Structural zeros, latent probability recovery, real-data performance, and universal geometric preferences are outside the evidence.\npaper.tex:33:\\caption{Study overview. Training data determine centers and local scales, including internal rank-based selection. A separate calibration sample determines conformal thresholds. Evaluation distinguishes membership of observed compositions from mass under a uniform-simplex reference distribution; neither estimand establishes conditional or shifted validity.}\npaper.tex:63:% @evidence[[literature/FanSesia2025.json#/excerpts/algorithms_S1_S2]]\npaper.tex:69:For each fitted instance, empirical coverage $\\widehat c_\\alpha$ is the fraction of test responses inside the region. Reliability error is $e_\\alpha=|\\widehat c_\\alpha-(1-\\alpha)|$, treating overcoverage and undercoverage symmetrically. Efficiency is the normalized uniform-simplex reference mass\npaper.tex:75:The implementation estimates this probability with shared probes and test contexts, not by integrating ILR-box volume. Thus $M_\\alpha\\in[0,1]$ is dimension-normalized simplex volume, not probability content under the response law. We use ``mass'' to keep the reference distribution explicit. Small mass alone does not establish informative calibrated prediction. % @evidence[[source_evidence/evaluate_final.py#generate_arrays]]\npaper.tex:86:\\textbf{Scaled ILR} uses Eq.~\\eqref{eq:score} with the fitted scales described below. \\textbf{Unscaled ILR} retains exactly the same center and basis but sets every scale to one; it removes the entire scaling package, not only the local correction. \\textbf{Simplex max} maps the common center through $h$ and calibrates $\\max_k|y_k-h_k(\\widehat\\mu(x))|$. \\textbf{Bonferroni} calibrates separate absolute residuals in each simplex coordinate at miscoverage $\\alpha/K$ and intersects the resulting intervals with the simplex. The latter is a simultaneous-coordinate baseline, not a collection of independently claimed marginal intervals. The unscaled joint-maximum and coordinatewise Bonferroni constructions are also described in multivariate conformal prediction \\citep{FanSesia2025}; here, both are intersected with the simplex. All thresholds use their own appropriate calibration residuals from the same calibration split. % @evidence[[source_evidence/evaluate_final.py#method_records]]\npaper.tex:137:Logistic-normal responses are $h(\\eta(X)+\\epsilon)$ with independent Gaussian coordinates of standard deviation 0.25. The Dirichlet construction uses parameter vector $\\tau(x)h(\\eta(x))$, with $\\tau(x)=4+35/(1+\\exp(2x_0))$. The generator draws gamma coordinates, floors them at $10^{-12}$, and closes the vector to sum to one. The evaluated law is this numerically floored distribution, not an unfloored boundary model. % @evidence[[source_evidence/evaluate_final.py#generate_arrays]]\npaper.tex:150:For the coverage curves, overall utility intervals, and feature-group mean coverage, we derive additional descriptive percentile intervals by jointly resampling seed blocks, preserving all condition and level records within a draw. The saved analysis uses 20,000 draws and bootstrap seed 20261117. These are analyses of retained measurements, not additional experiments. Per-seed Wilson intervals characterize test-membership sampling for one fitted/calibrated instance; we neither average their endpoints nor substitute them for cross-seed uncertainty. No confidence interval here captures arbitrary generator changes or adaptation to the held-out findings. % @evidence[[analysis/summary.json]]\npaper.tex:160:\\caption{Observed-composition coverage for scaled ILR. Left: $K=3$; right: $K=8$. Whiskers are descriptive 95\\% seed-block bootstrap intervals derived from retained observations. The truncated vertical axis highlights departures from the dashed nominal-coverage reference. IID behavior is close to target; the shifted Dirichlet deficit is substantial at every level. Lines connect nominal levels, not time.}\npaper.tex:184:Figure~\\ref{fig:paired} compares scaled ILR with simplex max. Positive utility differences and negative mass differences have different favorable directions, shown in separate columns. The largest favorable contrasts occur for IID Dirichlet responses at $K=8$. At $\\alpha=0.10$, utility increases by 0.1962 [0.1741, 0.2168], while mass decreases by 0.2024 with interval [$-0.2254$,$-0.1800$]. All reported nominal levels in that condition favor scaled ILR on both quantities. This localized pattern is stronger than the small aggregate utility difference, but it does not identify which part of the scaling construction is responsible. % @evidence[[analysis/summary.json#/retained_uncertainty/DIR_IID_K8/paired_baselines/simplex_linf]]\npaper.tex:194:The contrasts are not uniformly favorable even without shift. For IID Dirichlet $K=3$ at $\\alpha=0.20$, utility is lower by 0.0323 [$-0.0451$,$-0.0199$], and mass is larger by 0.0261. Several logistic-normal contrasts are small, with descriptive intervals including zero. These results reject a blanket preference for scaled ILR across response laws, dimensions, and target levels. % @evidence[[analysis/summary.json#/retained_uncertainty/DIR_IID_K3/paired_baselines/simplex_linf]]\npaper.tex:209:The mismatch between the selection objective and the evaluation reference measure is an explicit limitation of the construction. The result is compatible with that mismatch, but does not isolate it as the cause of unfavorable mass differences. The shift also jointly changes support, feature frequencies, and the frequency of high-variability responses. Its adverse coverage cannot be attributed solely to extrapolation, scale misspecification, or one design choice without additional controlled evidence. Standard exchangeability theory explains why no unchanged guarantee is available, not the magnitude of the observed deficit.\npaper.tex:215:The study has no public-data experiment, alternative ILR basis, learned covariance region, structural-zero response, or independent boundary-face reference measure. Positivity flooring makes logratios computable but is not a principled solution for true zeros. Uniform-simplex mass is a clearly defined reference measure, not a universal notion of user-relevant uncertainty. Its dimension dependence and finite probe resolution can hide small or boundary-concentrated regions. Conversely, a smaller mass need not imply better prediction for the actual response law. These limitations prevent conclusions about classification label sets, latent probability regions, or general deployment reliability.\npaper.tex:218:The evidence recommends an evaluation discipline rather than a universal method choice: specify the response being covered, state the reference measure, compare matched configurations, retain condition-specific failures, and distinguish marginal from conditional and shifted validity. Aggregate utility can be useful for summarization only after its components are visible. Where support changes or calibration is reused for selection, assumptions must be reassessed rather than inferred from an apparently favorable score. Neither issue is repaired by a publication decision or by satisfying a completion check.\npaper.tex:226:\\bibliography{references}\npaper.tex:227:\\bibliographystyle{iclr2026_conference}\npaper.tex:248:Training, calibration, test, and reference-probe random streams are separated using \\texttt{SeedSequence([seed, case\\_index, split\\_index, 202611])}; the reference stream has split index 3. The manifest stores array and row provenance and the evaluator checks normalized float64 hashes, positive responses, closure, feature shapes, and split-content disjointness. The retained manifest is \\texttt{source\\_evidence/data\\_manifest.json}; hashes identify the retained data but do not establish substantive methodological properties. The response is always the observed continuous vector; no latent target or categorical observation is substituted. % @evidence[[source_evidence/evaluate_final.py#load_resources]]\npaper.tex:250:\\textbf{Environment and commands.} The evaluated implementation requires CPython 3.11 or 3.12 and NumPy 1.26.4, uses CPU computation, and has no downloaded dataset, pretrained model, or external data license requirement. The native evaluator additionally requires Linux process-isolation facilities documented in \\texttt{source\\_evidence/task\\_contract.json}; a portable re-execution should not silently bypass those controls. \\texttt{REPRODUCIBILITY.md} records the evaluator command, complete retained-data analysis commands, and artifact mappings. The plotting environment used here is recorded separately in \\texttt{analysis/environment.json}; it is not represented as the historical evaluation environment. Runtime is not estimated from plotting time, and the final evaluation was not rerun during manuscript preparation. % @evidence[[source_evidence/requirements.txt]]\npaper.tex:300:Dir K3 & 0.10 & +0.031 & [+0.018,+0.043] & [+0.010,+0.050] & -0.041 & [-0.056,-0.026] \\\\\npaper.tex:301:Dir K3 & 0.20 & -0.026 & [-0.036,-0.015] & [-0.040,-0.008] & +0.019 & [+0.009,+0.029] \\\\\npaper.tex:303:Dir K8 & 0.10 & +0.300 & [+0.287,+0.315] & [+0.280,+0.326] & -0.306 & [-0.325,-0.289] \\\\\npaper.tex:305:Shift K3 & 0.05 & -0.025 & [-0.046,-0.005] & [-0.059,+0.007] & +0.052 & [+0.019,+0.088] \\\\\npaper.tex:310:Shift K8 & 0.20 & -0.007 & [-0.024,+0.019] & [-0.028,+0.041] & +0.172 & [+0.108,+0.226] \\\\\npaper.tex:321:LN K3 & 0.05 & +0.017 & [+0.006,+0.028] & [+0.003,+0.036] & -0.017 & [-0.025,-0.010] \\\\\npaper.tex:329:Dir K3 & 0.20 & -0.032 & [-0.045,-0.020] & [-0.053,-0.013] & +0.026 & [+0.017,+0.036] \\\\\npaper.tex:331:Dir K8 & 0.10 & +0.196 & [+0.174,+0.217] & [+0.158,+0.229] & -0.202 & [-0.225,-0.180] \\\\\npaper.tex:333:Shift K3 & 0.05 & -0.079 & [-0.094,-0.067] & [-0.104,-0.063] & +0.095 & [+0.071,+0.125] \\\\\npaper.tex:338:Shift K8 & 0.20 & -0.058 & [-0.074,-0.040] & [-0.081,-0.025] & +0.232 & [+0.189,+0.273] \\\\\npaper.tex:350:LN K3 & 0.10 & +0.029 & [+0.023,+0.038] & [+0.021,+0.046] & -0.021 & [-0.026,-0.016] \\\\\npaper.tex:352:LN K8 & 0.05 & +0.026 & [+0.021,+0.032] & [+0.018,+0.036] & -0.026 & [-0.031,-0.021] \\\\\npaper.tex:354:LN K8 & 0.20 & +0.015 & [+0.006,+0.025] & [+0.002,+0.031] & -0.003 & [-0.004,-0.003] \\\\\npaper.tex:358:Dir K8 & 0.05 & +0.246 & [+0.207,+0.285] & [+0.182,+0.308] & -0.250 & [-0.291,-0.211] \\\\\npaper.tex:359:Dir K8 & 0.10 & +0.216 & [+0.192,+0.242] & [+0.178,+0.259] & -0.222 & [-0.250,-0.197] \\\\\npaper.tex:360:Dir K8 & 0.20 & +0.146 & [+0.131,+0.160] & [+0.118,+0.169] & -0.139 & [-0.155,-0.126] \\\\\npaper.tex:382:The constant-input control replaces each query feature vector by the training-feature mean before prediction. The permuted-input control rotates calibration rows and test rows separately, disrupting feature-response alignment without transferring labels. The constant-center reference uses the training mean in ILR coordinates and unit scales. These controls change information available at prediction time or the center itself; they are not interchangeable with the matched-center unscaled comparison. The evaluator supplies feature-control paired utility intervals in the retained uncertainty object. We do not use passing controls as evidence that every scientific validity threat has been excluded. % @evidence[[source_evidence/evaluate_final.py#evaluate_episode]]\npaper.tex:387:The whole-simplex construction has unit reference mass and covers every observed composition; its utility is zero because the efficiency factor vanishes. The zero-radius reference tests equality with a fitted point, not recovery of the original observation. These endpoints check that metric behavior is interpretable, but do not supply an efficiency comparison among useful calibrated sets. No separate integration over simplex faces or boundary strata is present. All measured mass refers to the full-dimensional uniform-simplex reference distribution. % @evidence[[source_evidence/evaluate_final.py#method_records]]\npaper.tex:397:Language models assisted hypothesis formulation, training-only method design, implementation preparation, reference-query planning, synthesis of retained measurements, manuscript drafting, and construction of plotting and validation scripts. The manuscript-writing assistant inspected archived implementations and final records, generated figures from existing measurements, and retrieved exact foundational references for the cited background. It did not generate new scientific evaluation episodes. The overview figure is prepared through an automated concept-figure workflow; quantitative plots are deterministic visualizations of retained records. No model-generated number is used as an empirical result. No human-supervision procedure or independent replication is asserted beyond the documented checks. The process logs are retained for audit without identifying authors.\nREPRODUCIBILITY.md:12:- `source_evidence/requirements.txt`: NumPy 1.26.4, matching the historical evaluator requirement. CPython 3.11 or 3.12 is required.\nREPRODUCIBILITY.md:39:The actual analysis environment versions are recorded in `analysis/environment.json`; these are not asserted to be the historical research environment. The original numerical study requires its separate pinned NumPy environment. `build_analysis.py` reads all measurements from the final evidence, never the development scores. Its additional descriptive intervals use 20000 joint seed-block draws at seed 20261117. Stored paired intervals are copied once from the duplicated evaluator uncertainty object; they are not recomputed as independent observations.\nREPRODUCIBILITY.md:49:| Conceptual overview | Orchestrator-owned `generate-concept-figure` workflow | Strategy and manuscript, no invented measurements |\nREPRODUCIBILITY.md:50:| Claim map | `build_claims.py` -> `claims_grounding.json` | Registry, readable implementation evidence, derived summaries, references |\nREPRODUCIBILITY.md:52:Each quantitative PDF also has a PNG preview and an adjacent provenance sidecar. The overview PNG and empty prompt-log stub are intentional pre-orchestration placeholders. Its reusable placeholder code skips creation if a render marker or nonempty prompt log exists, and never overwrites an existing image. The final synchronous concept render is owned by the surrounding workflow, not by `build_analysis.py`.\nREPRODUCIBILITY.md:55:`retrieve_exact_references.py` retrieves only named foundational works or direct known arXiv identifiers; it does not perform a joint conformal/composition literature search, traverse citation networks, or look for the prohibited related study. `literature/` records actual consulted abstracts and publisher excerpts. The manuscript cites only the 25 retained entries in `references.bib`; `references_provenance.json` records access scope. Initial identifier and DOI mismatches returned unrelated bibliographic material, which was discarded rather than cited. No prohibited study was identified or used, and no citation-network traversal was performed. Abstract consultation supports only broad background descriptions, not unverified theorem details. The split-conformal rank argument is given directly in the text.\nREPRODUCIBILITY.md:61:python .codex/skills/validate-paper/scripts/validate_tex_refs.py --tex paper.tex --bib references.bib\n--- before/paper.tex\n+++ after/paper.tex\n@@ -60,6 +60,7 @@\n \\end{equation}\n % @evidence[[source_evidence/selected_model.py#_basis]]\n % @evidence[[source_evidence/evaluate_final.py#method_records]]\n+% @evidence[[literature/FanSesia2025.json#/excerpts/algorithms_S1_S2]]\n Given $m$ independent calibration scores, use the $r$th smallest score with $r=\\lceil(m+1)(1-\\alpha)\\rceil$, or $+\\infty$ if $r>m$. The region consists of positive compositions satisfying $A(x,y)\\leq q_\\alpha$. It is an axis-aligned box in the fixed ILR basis, mapped back to the simplex; it is not a full-covariance ellipsoid. The finite-rank rule, including its infinite-threshold case, is owned by the evaluation code rather than the fitted predictor. % @evidence[[source_evidence/evaluate_final.py#quantile]]\n \n Conditional on the training data, exchangeable calibration and test scores give $\\Pr\\{Y\\in C_\\alpha(X)\\}\\geq1-\\alpha$ by the standard conformal rank argument \\citep{Lei2018}. Ties are handled conservatively by the non-strict membership inequality. This is a statement about a random calibration sample and future response, not a guarantee for every realized test set, every fixed feature vector, or every group. Training-only adaptation leaves this argument intact because the final calibration responses do not enter score selection. The argument does not apply unchanged to our shifted test sample.\n@@ -82,7 +83,7 @@\n It rewards small regions and penalizes coverage error but is not a coverage constraint. A method can improve $U_\\alpha$ by trading mass against error; its ordering need not agree with either component. We therefore report all components and treat utility as a supplied descriptive summary, not a proof of scientific superiority. Overall utility averages conditions and nominal levels equally. IID and shifted errors are averaged separately, after taking absolute deviations within each seed, condition, and level. Taking the absolute value after averaging coverage would produce a different estimand. % @evidence[[source_evidence/evaluate_final.py#aggregate]]\n \n \\subsection{Compared region constructions}\n-\\textbf{Scaled ILR} uses Eq.~\\eqref{eq:score} with the fitted scales described below. \\textbf{Unscaled ILR} retains exactly the same center and basis but sets every scale to one; it removes the entire scaling package, not only the local correction. \\textbf{Simplex max} maps the common center through $h$ and calibrates $\\max_k|y_k-h_k(\\widehat\\mu(x))|$. \\textbf{Bonferroni} calibrates separate absolute residuals in each simplex coordinate at miscoverage $\\alpha/K$ and intersects the resulting intervals with the simplex. The latter is a simultaneous-coordinate baseline, not a collection of independently claimed marginal intervals. All thresholds use their own appropriate calibration residuals from the same calibration split. % @evidence[[source_evidence/evaluate_final.py#method_records]]\n+\\textbf{Scaled ILR} uses Eq.~\\eqref{eq:score} with the fitted scales described below. \\textbf{Unscaled ILR} retains exactly the same center and basis but sets every scale to one; it removes the entire scaling package, not only the local correction. \\textbf{Simplex max} maps the common center through $h$ and calibrates $\\max_k|y_k-h_k(\\widehat\\mu(x))|$. \\textbf{Bonferroni} calibrates separate absolute residuals in each simplex coordinate at miscoverage $\\alpha/K$ and intersects the resulting intervals with the simplex. The latter is a simultaneous-coordinate baseline, not a collection of independently claimed marginal intervals. The unscaled joint-maximum and coordinatewise Bonferroni constructions are also described in multivariate conformal prediction \\citep{FanSesia2025}; here, both are intersected with the simplex. All thresholds use their own appropriate calibration residuals from the same calibration split. % @evidence[[source_evidence/evaluate_final.py#method_records]]\n \n These comparisons hold center predictions fixed while changing the residual geometry or scaling. The unscaled comparison can support conclusions about the complete scaling construction within this evaluation. It cannot identify the effect of local smoothing, cross-fitting, shrinkage, or the selector individually. The simplex comparison additionally changes coordinates, so any observed contrast is a comparison between region constructions rather than a causal estimate for one isolated component.\n \n\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
